(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }

  var SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM = {};
  SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM.som = [
    P("Define tensile stress.", "Tensile stress σ = Force/Area = P/A. It is the resisting force per unit area developed when a body is subjected to axial tensile load.", "Stress equals load divided by cross-sectional area.", "When a bar is pulled, the internal resisting force per unit original cross-sectional area is tensile stress. SI unit is N/m² or Pa. For a bar of area A under load P, σ = P/A. If the bar elongates, the stress is tensile."),
    P("Define tensile strain.", "Tensile strain ε = Change in length / Original length = ΔL/L. It is dimensionless.", "Strain is the ratio of deformation to original dimension.", "When a bar of original length L is stretched to L + ΔL, the tensile strain is ε = ΔL/L. Strain has no unit. For a bar under axial load, ε = σ/E where E is Young's modulus."),
    P("Define compressive stress and compressive strain.", "Compressive stress σ_c = P/A (compressive load). Compressive strain ε_c = ΔL/L (decrease in length per unit original length).", "Same formulas as tension, but the load causes shortening.", "Compressive stress develops when external forces tend to shorten the body. For a column of area A carrying load P in compression, σ_c = P/A. The corresponding strain is ε_c = δL/L where δL is the decrease in length."),
    P("Define shear stress.", "Shear stress τ = Tangential force / Area = F/A. It acts parallel to the cross-sectional area.", "Shear stress acts along (parallel to) the surface, not perpendicular.", "When two equal and opposite forces act tangentially on a section, shear stress develops. For a rivet of diameter d resisting force F, τ = F/(πd²/4). SI unit is Pa or N/m²."),
    P("Define shear strain.", "Shear strain γ = Angular deformation = tan φ ≈ φ (for small angles), where φ is the angle through which a right angle changes.", "Think of the angular distortion of a rectangular element.", "When a rectangular block is sheared, the right angle at a corner changes by angle φ. The shear strain γ = φ ≈ tan φ for small deformations. γ is dimensionless. Related to shear stress by τ = Gγ."),
    P("State Hooke's law of elasticity.", "Within the elastic limit, stress is directly proportional to strain: σ = Eε. The constant of proportionality E is Young's modulus.", "Hooke's law is the linear stress-strain region.", "Hooke's law states that for a material within the proportional limit, stress ∝ strain, i.e., σ = Eε. E = Stress/Strain = (P/A)/(ΔL/L). This law is valid only up to the proportional limit."),
    P("Define Young's modulus E.", "E = Longitudinal stress / Longitudinal strain = (P/A)/(ΔL/L) = PL/(AΔL). It measures stiffness in tension or compression.", "E = stress/strain in the axial direction.", "Young's modulus E quantifies resistance to elastic deformation under axial loading. For steel, E ≈ 200 GPa; for aluminium, E ≈ 70 GPa; for copper, E ≈ 120 GPa. Unit is Pa or N/m²."),
    P("What is the difference between proportional limit and elastic limit?", "Proportional limit: maximum stress up to which stress-strain curve is linear. Elastic limit: maximum stress up to which the material returns to its original shape. Elastic limit ≥ proportional limit.", "Proportional limit is where linearity ends; elastic limit is where permanent set begins.", "At the proportional limit, the stress-strain graph is linear. The elastic limit is slightly higher — the material recovers fully but the relationship is no longer perfectly linear. For most engineering purposes, these two limits are approximately equal."),
    P("Define Bulk modulus K.", "K = Volumetric stress / Volumetric strain = –p / (ΔV/V). It measures resistance to uniform compression.", "Bulk modulus relates hydrostatic pressure to volume change.", "When a body is subjected to uniform hydrostatic pressure p, the volumetric strain is ΔV/V. K = –p/(ΔV/V). For water, K ≈ 2.2 GPa; for steel, K ≈ 160 GPa. Higher K means less volume change under pressure."),
    P("Define Shear modulus G (modulus of rigidity).", "G = Shear stress / Shear strain = τ/γ. It measures resistance to shear deformation.", "G relates shear stress to the angular distortion.", "When a material is sheared, the ratio of shear stress to shear strain gives G = τ/γ. For steel, G ≈ 80 GPa; for aluminium, G ≈ 27 GPa. G is always less than E for any material."),
    P("What is the relation between E, G, and Poisson's ratio ν?", "E = 2G(1 + ν). This is the fundamental relation connecting Young's modulus and shear modulus through Poisson's ratio.", "Use the generalised Hooke's law derivation.", "From generalized Hooke's law: G = E/(2(1 + ν)). Rearranging: E = 2G(1 + ν). For ν = 0.3: E = 2.6G, so G = E/2.6 ≈ 0.385E."),
    P("What is the relation between E, K, and Poisson's ratio ν?", "E = 3K(1 – 2ν). This connects Young's modulus with bulk modulus through Poisson's ratio.", "Derive from volumetric strain in terms of linear strains.", "For hydrostatic pressure: volumetric strain = –3p(1 – 2ν)/E. Since K = –p/(ΔV/V), we obtain E = 3K(1 – 2ν). This is valid for isotropic elastic materials."),
    P("What is the relation between G, K, and ν?", "G = 3K(1 – 2ν) / (2(1 + ν)) or K = 2G(1 + ν) / (3(1 – 2ν)).", "Eliminate E from E = 2G(1+ν) and E = 3K(1–2ν).", "Setting 2G(1 + ν) = 3K(1 – 2ν) gives G/K = 3(1 – 2ν)/(2(1 + ν)). For ν = 0.3: G/K ≈ 0.462. All five quantities E, G, K, λ (Lamé constant), ν are interrelated."),
    P("For ν = 0.3, find G/E and K/E.", "G/E = 1/(2(1+ν)) = 1/2.6 ≈ 0.385. K/E = 1/(3(1–2ν)) = 1/1.2 ≈ 0.833.", "Substitute ν = 0.3 into the standard relations.", "Using E = 2G(1+ν): G = E/2.6, so G/E ≈ 0.3846. Using E = 3K(1–2ν): K = E/1.2, so K/E ≈ 0.8333. These ratios are frequently asked in SSC-JE exams."),
    P("What is the theoretical range of Poisson's ratio for isotropic materials?", "–1 ≤ ν ≤ 0.5. For most engineering metals, ν ranges from 0.25 to 0.35. For rubber, ν ≈ 0.5. For cork, ν ≈ 0.", "The limits come from thermodynamic stability: G > 0 and K > 0.", "From G = E/(2(1+ν)) > 0: ν > –1. From K = E/(3(1–2ν)) > 0: ν < 0.5. Practical values: steel ν ≈ 0.28–0.30, concrete ν ≈ 0.15–0.20, rubber ν ≈ 0.499."),
    P("What is Poisson's ratio for cork and why is it special?", "Poisson's ratio of cork is approximately 0. It has nearly zero lateral strain when compressed axially, making it ideal for bottle stoppers.", "Cork's cellular structure collapses inward rather than expanding laterally.", "Cork has ν ≈ 0 because its honeycomb-like cellular structure collapses inward under compression rather than expanding laterally. This makes it ideal for bottle stoppers — it compresses easily without pushing against the bottle walls."),
    P("Define proof resilience.", "Proof resilience is the maximum strain energy that can be stored in a material without permanent deformation. PR = σ²_el / (2E) per unit volume.", "Proof resilience = area under stress-strain curve up to elastic limit.", "When loaded to the elastic limit σ_el, the strain energy per unit volume is PR = ½ × σ_el × ε_el = σ²_el/(2E). For mild steel with σ_el ≈ 250 MPa, E = 200 GPa: PR = 156.25 kJ/m³."),
    P("Define modulus of resilience.", "Modulus of resilience = strain energy per unit volume at the elastic limit: MR = σ²_el / (2E). Same as proof resilience per unit volume.", "MR = ½ × σ_el × ε_el = σ²_el/(2E).", "A material with high MR can absorb more energy before yielding. For mild steel: MR = (250×10⁶)²/(2 × 200×10⁹) = 156.25 J/m³. MR is an important property for applications requiring energy absorption."),
    P("Define toughness of a material.", "Toughness is the total energy per unit volume a material can absorb before fracture. It equals the total area under the stress-strain curve up to fracture point.", "Toughness = total area under the complete stress-strain diagram.", "Unlike resilience (up to elastic limit), toughness is the energy up to fracture. Tough materials have high strength and ductility. For mild steel, toughness ≈ 50–100 MJ/m³. Toughness is important for impact-resistant applications."),
    P("What is the expression for strain energy in a bar under axial load?", "U = P²L/(2AE) = (σ² × Volume)/(2E) = ½ × P × ΔL.", "Strain energy = average force × displacement = ½ P × ΔL.", "Under gradual axial loading, the bar elongates by ΔL = PL/(AE). The work done = ½ × P × ΔL = P²L/(2AE). This is stored as strain energy U. Per unit volume: u = σ²/(2E) = ½ σε."),
    P("What is the strain energy in a beam due to bending?", "U = ∫ M²/(2EI) dx over the beam length. For constant M over length L: U = M²L/(2EI).", "Integrate bending strain energy density over the beam.", "The strain energy per unit length due to bending is dU/dx = M²/(2EI), where M = bending moment, E = Young's modulus, I = moment of inertia. Total U = ∫₀ᴸ M²/(2EI) dx. This is used in Castigliano's theorem."),
    P("What is the strain energy due to torsion?", "U = T²L/(2GJ) = τ² × Volume / (2G) for a uniform shaft. T = torque, L = length, G = shear modulus, J = polar moment.", "Similar to axial case but using torque and polar moment.", "Under torque T, the shaft twists by θ = TL/(GJ). The strain energy is U = ½ × T × θ = T²L/(2GJ). Per unit volume: u = τ²/(2G). For a hollow shaft, J = π(D⁴ – d⁴)/32."),
    P("Define thermal stress.", "Thermal stress σ_th = E × α × ΔT. It develops when free expansion or contraction is prevented. α = coefficient of thermal expansion.", "Thermal stress = E × thermal strain = E × α × ΔT.", "When temperature changes by ΔT, free expansion = αLΔT. If fully restrained, thermal strain = –αΔT, and stress = –EαΔT (compressive if heated). For steel (E = 200 GPa, α = 12×10⁻⁶/°C) with ΔT = 50°C: σ = 120 MPa."),
    P("What is the formula for free thermal expansion?", "Free expansion ΔL = L × α × ΔT. Free volumetric expansion ΔV = V × β × ΔT, where β ≈ 3α for isotropic materials.", "Thermal expansion is linear in temperature change and original length.", "When a bar of length L is heated through ΔT with no constraints, it expands freely by ΔL = αLΔT. No stress develops. The volume expansion coefficient β = 3α. α for steel = 12×10⁻⁶/°C, aluminium = 23×10⁻⁶/°C."),
    P("What is the thermal stress in a bar fixed at both ends when temperature rises?", "σ_th = EαΔT (compressive). Both ends being fixed means zero elongation; the supports exert a compressive reaction.", "Zero total elongation: ΔL_thermal + ΔL_force = 0.", "Free expansion: ΔL_T = αLΔT. Compressive force P prevents this: PL/(AE) = αLΔT → P = EAαΔT → σ = EαΔT (compressive). If temperature drops, the stress is tensile with the same magnitude."),
    P("What is thermal strain?", "Thermal strain ε_th = α × ΔT. It is the strain that would occur if the body were free to expand. If restrained, thermal stress develops.", "Thermal strain is the free expansion per unit length.", "When temperature increases by ΔT, the material wants to expand by αΔT per unit length. This is the thermal strain. If free, this strain occurs with zero stress. If fully restrained, mechanical strain = –αΔT and stress = –EαΔT."),
    P("What is a compound bar?", "A compound bar consists of two or more bars of different materials joined together, either in parallel or in series, to carry axial loads.", "Compound bars: multiple materials sharing load under common deformation conditions.", "When two bars (e.g., steel and brass) are bonded in parallel and loaded axially: (1) Both undergo equal strain: ε₁ = ε₂. (2) Total load: P = P₁ + P₂ = σ₁A₁ + σ₂A₂. Using σ = Eε: P = ε(E₁A₁ + E₂A₂). Stresses differ: σ₁/σ₂ = E₁/E₂."),
    P("What are the conditions for two bars in parallel under axial load?", "Equal strain: ε₁ = ε₂. Load sharing: P = P₁ + P₂. Stress ratio: σ₁/σ₂ = E₁/E₂. Equivalent EA: (EA)_eq = E₁A₁ + E₂A₂.", "Bars in parallel share load based on their stiffness EA.", "Two bars bonded in parallel have identical elongation ΔL. Strain ε = ΔL/L is the same. Force in bar 1: P₁ = E₁A₁ε. Total: P = (E₁A₁ + E₂A₂)ε. So σ₁ = E₁P/(E₁A₁ + E₂A₂) and σ₂ = E₂P/(E₁A₁ + E₂A₂)."),
    P("What is the stress in each material of a steel-brass compound bar in parallel?", "σ_steel = E_s × P/(E_sA_s + E_bA_b), σ_brass = E_b × P/(E_sA_s + E_bA_b). Steel carries a larger share of the stress.", "Higher E material attracts more stress in parallel arrangement.", "Since both bars have equal strain, σ_steel/E_s = σ_brass/E_b. Steel (E = 200 GPa) attracts more stress than brass (E ≈ 100 GPa) in a ratio of 2:1. The stiffer material always carries a proportionally larger share of the total load."),
    P("What is the extension of a uniform bar under its own weight?", "ΔL = γL²/(2E) = WL/(2AE), where W = weight of the bar, γ = specific weight, L = length, A = cross-sectional area.", "Each element contributes extension due to the weight below it.", "For a bar hanging under self-weight, the force at distance x from the free end is Wx/L. The extension of element dx is dΔ = (Wx/L)dx/(AE). Integrating: ΔL = WL/(2AE) = γL²/(2E). This is half the extension if the full weight were applied at the free end."),
    P("What is the extension of a tapering bar (circular section) under axial load?", "ΔL = 4PL/(πE d₁ d₂), where d₁ and d₂ are the end diameters, P = axial load, L = length.", "The diameter varies linearly; integrate over the length.", "For a tapering bar with diameters d₁ and d₂ at the two ends: A(x) = (π/4)[d₁ + (d₂–d₁)x/L]². Extension = ∫P dx/(E A(x)). Integrating: ΔL = 4PL/(πE d₁ d₂). For d₁ = d₂ = d (uniform bar): ΔL = 4PL/(πEd²) = PL/(AE)."),
    P("What is the difference between a column and a strut?", "A column is a long, slender compression member that fails by buckling (Euler). A strut is a short, thick compression member that fails by crushing (yielding).", "Columns buckle; struts crush. The slenderness ratio distinguishes them.", "A column has a high slenderness ratio (Le/k > critical) and fails by elastic buckling before reaching the yield stress. A strut has a low slenderness ratio (Le/k < critical) and fails by crushing. For intermediate members, Rankine's formula applies. In practice, 'column' and 'strut' are often used interchangeably in buildings."),
    P("What is complementary shear stress?", "Complementary shear stresses are equal in magnitude, act on mutually perpendicular planes, and act either both towards or both away from the line of intersection of the planes.", "An equal shear stress appears on the perpendicular face to maintain equilibrium.", "When shear stress τ acts on one face, an equal shear stress τ acts on the perpendicular face to maintain rotational equilibrium. These are complementary shear stresses, equal in magnitude and acting in the same rotational sense. This principle is fundamental to Mohr's circle."),
    P("State the property of complementary shear stress.", "Complementary shear stresses on two perpendicular planes are always equal in magnitude and act in the same rotational sense.", "Equal and same rotational sense on perpendicular faces.", "From moment equilibrium of a small element: τ_xy × dy × dx = τ_yx × dx × dy → τ_xy = τ_yx. The magnitude of shear stress on any plane equals the shear stress on the perpendicular plane. This is used to derive the stress transformation equations."),
    P("What is the relation between shear stress τ and shear strain γ in the elastic range?", "τ = Gγ, where G is the modulus of rigidity. This is Hooke's law for shear.", "Shear stress equals shear modulus times shear strain.", "Within the elastic limit, τ = Gγ. The modulus of rigidity G = τ/γ = E/(2(1+ν)). For mild steel, G ≈ 80 GPa. The shear yield stress is approximately 0.577 times the tensile yield stress (von Mises criterion)."),
    P("Define principal stresses.", "Principal stresses are the maximum and minimum normal stresses acting on planes where the shear stress is zero. In 2D: σ₁,₂ = (σ_x + σ_y)/2 ± √[((σ_x – σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²].", "Principal planes are those with zero shear stress.", "When an element is subjected to combined loading, there exist two mutually perpendicular planes with zero shear stress, on which normal stresses are maximum and minimum. These are the principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂. In plane stress, σ₃ = 0."),
    P("Write the formulas for principal stresses for a 2D stress element.", "σ₁,₂ = (σ_x + σ_y)/2 ± √[((σ_x – σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²]. Angle of principal planes: tan(2θ_p) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x – σ_y).", "Use the stress transformation equations and set dσ/dθ = 0.", "For an element with σ_x, σ_y, and τ_xy, principal stresses come from transformation equations. The maximum and minimum normal stresses occur at θ_p where tan(2θ_p) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x – σ_y). At these planes the shear stress is zero."),
    P("What is the maximum shear stress in terms of principal stresses (3D)?", "τ_max = (σ₁ – σ₃)/2, where σ₁ ≥ σ₂ ≥ σ₃. In plane stress (σ₃ = 0), the in-plane max shear = (σ₁ – σ₂)/2.", "Maximum shear stress is half the difference of the extreme principal stresses.", "The three Mohr's circles have diameters (σ₁–σ₂), (σ₂–σ₃), (σ₁–σ₃). The largest circle gives τ_max = (σ₁ – σ₃)/2. The plane of maximum shear is at 45° to the principal planes."),
    P("What are principal strains?", "Principal strains are the maximum and minimum normal strains occurring on planes of zero shear strain. ε₁,₂ = (ε_x + ε_y)/2 ± √[((ε_x – ε_y)/2)² + (γ_xy/2)²].", "Principal strains occur on the same planes as principal stresses.", "Principal strains are the strain counterparts of principal stresses. The angle of principal strain planes: tan(2θ_p) = γ_xy/(ε_x – ε_y). For uniaxial test: ε₁ = σ/E, ε₂ = ε₃ = –νσ/E."),
    P("What are the formulas for principal strains in terms of principal stresses?", "ε₁ = (σ₁ – ν(σ₂ + σ₃))/E, ε₂ = (σ₂ – ν(σ₁ + σ₃))/E, ε₃ = (σ₃ – ν(σ₁ + σ₂))/E.", "Apply generalized Hooke's law along each principal direction.", "In principal directions there are no shear stresses. For plane stress (σ₃ = 0): ε₁ = (σ₁ – νσ₂)/E, ε₂ = (σ₂ – νσ₁)/E. Volumetric strain ε_v = ε₁ + ε₂ + ε₃ = (1 – 2ν)(σ₁ + σ₂ + σ₃)/E."),
    P("What is the maximum shear strain in terms of principal strains?", "γ_max = ε₁ – ε₃, where ε₁ ≥ ε₂ ≥ ε₃ are the principal strains. Related to max shear stress by γ_max = τ_max/G.", "Maximum shear strain = difference of extreme principal strains.", "The maximum shear strain occurs on a plane at 45° to the principal planes and equals ε₁ – ε₃. It is related to maximum shear stress by γ_max = τ_max/G = (σ₁ – σ₃)/(2G)."),
    P("What is the relation between maximum shear and normal stress on the plane of max in-plane shear?", "τ_max(in-plane) = (σ₁ – σ₂)/2. The normal stress on the plane of max shear = (σ₁ + σ₂)/2 = σ_avg.", "Max shear plane is at 45° to principal planes and carries the average normal stress.", "The radius of Mohr's circle equals τ_max = (σ₁ – σ₂)/2. On this plane, the normal stress equals the centre of Mohr's circle: σ_n = (σ₁ + σ₂)/2. The maximum shear plane is at θ_s = θ_p ± 45° from the principal plane."),
    P("What does Mohr's circle represent?", "Mohr's circle graphically represents normal and shear stresses on any inclined plane at a point. Normal stress is plotted on the x-axis and shear stress on the y-axis.", "Mohr's circle is a graphical solution of the stress transformation equations.", "For a 2D stress state (σ_x, σ_y, τ_xy): Centre C = ((σ_x+σ_y)/2, 0), Radius R = √[((σ_x–σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²]. Any point on the circle gives (σ, τ) on a plane at angle θ. Principal stresses: σ₁ = C + R, σ₂ = C – R."),
    P("What is the radius and centre of Mohr's circle?", "Centre: C = (σ_x + σ_y)/2. Radius: R = √[((σ_x – σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²]. σ₁ = C + R, σ₂ = C – R.", "Centre is the average normal stress; radius equals max in-plane shear stress.", "Mohr's circle is centred at ((σ_x+σ_y)/2, 0). The radius R = √[((σ_x–σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²] equals the maximum in-plane shear stress. Points A(σ_x, τ_xy) and B(σ_y, –τ_xy) are diametrically opposite."),
    P("How is the orientation of principal planes found from Mohr's circle?", "The angle 2θ_p on the circle is measured from the reference point (σ_x, τ_xy) to the principal point; the physical angle is θ_p = (2θ_p)/2.", "On Mohr's circle, angles are doubled; divide by 2 for the physical plane.", "From point A(σ_x, τ_xy) to the major principal stress point (σ₁, 0), the circle angle is 2θ_p where tan(2θ_p) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x – σ_y). The physical plane is rotated by θ_p = (2θ_p)/2 in the same direction."),
    P("What is the maximum in-plane shear stress formula?", "τ_max(in-plane) = R = √[((σ_x – σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²] = (σ₁ – σ₂)/2. The associated normal stress = (σ₁ + σ₂)/2.", "Maximum in-plane shear stress equals the radius of Mohr's circle.", "The maximum in-plane shear stress equals the radius of Mohr's circle: τ_max = √[((σ_x–σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²]. It occurs on planes at 45° to the principal planes. The normal stress on these planes is σ_avg = (σ_x+σ_y)/2."),
    P("What is the formula for hoop stress in a thin cylinder?", "σ_h = pd/(2t) for internal gauge pressure p, diameter d, wall thickness t. Hoop stress acts along the circumference.", "Hoop stress is twice the longitudinal stress in a thin cylinder.", "For a thin cylinder (d/t > 20), hoop stress is assumed uniform: σ_h = pd/(2t). This stress tries to burst the cylinder along its length. It is the critical stress. Longitudinal stress σ_L = pd/(4t)."),
    P("What is the formula for longitudinal stress in a thin cylinder?", "σ_L = pd/(4t), where p = pressure, d = internal diameter, t = wall thickness. This acts along the cylinder axis.", "Longitudinal stress is half the hoop stress for thin cylinders.", "The pressure on the end caps gives force = p × πd²/4. Resisted by the wall: σ_L × πd × t = pπd²/4 → σ_L = pd/(4t). σ_L = σ_h/2."),
    P("What is the ratio of hoop stress to longitudinal stress in a thin cylinder?", "2:1. σ_h = pd/(2t) is twice σ_L = pd/(4t).", "A frequently asked SSC-JE fact: hoop stress = 2 × longitudinal stress.", "In a thin cylinder under internal pressure: σ_h/σ_L = (pd/2t)/(pd/4t) = 2. The cylinder is more likely to fail by splitting along its length (hoop stress) than by separation at the ends."),
    P("What is Lame's equation for thick cylinders?", "σ_h = A + B/r², σ_r = A – B/r², where A and B are constants from boundary conditions at inner and outer radii.", "Lame's theory gives the exact stress distribution across the wall.", "For internal pressure p_i at r_i and external p_o at r_o: A = (p_ir_i² – p_or_o²)/(r_o² – r_i²), B = r_i²r_o²(p_i – p_o)/(r_o² – r_i²). For p_o = 0, max hoop stress is at the inner surface: σ_h(max) = p_i(r_o² + r_i²)/(r_o² – r_i²)."),
    P("What is the maximum hoop stress at the inner surface of a thick cylinder under internal pressure only?", "σ_h(max) = p(r_o² + r_i²)/(r_o² – r_i²) at r = r_i. The hoop stress at the outer surface = 2pr_i²/(r_o² – r_i²).", "Maximum stress is at the inner surface in thick cylinder theory.", "For internal pressure p with no external pressure, at r = r_i: σ_h = p(r_o² + r_i²)/(r_o² – r_i²), σ_r = –p. As r_o/r_i → ∞, σ_h(max) → p. For a solid cylinder, this shows the bore stress is always higher than the applied pressure."),
    P("What is the difference between thin and thick cylinder analysis?", "Thin (d/t > 20): uniform stress, σ_h = pd/(2t). Thick (d/t ≤ 20): Lame's equations give stress variation through the wall, maximum at the inner surface.", "Thin cylinder uses average stress; thick cylinder gives exact radial distribution.", "Thin cylinder theory assumes uniform stress across the wall for d/t > 20. Thick cylinder theory (Lame) accounts for stress variation through the wall. Thick analysis is needed for pressure vessels, gun barrels, and hydraulic cylinders."),
    P("What is the maximum stress in a thin spherical shell under internal pressure?", "σ = pd/(4t) for thin spherical shell, same in all tangential directions.", "Spherical shell stress is half the hoop stress of a cylinder.", "For a thin spherical shell under pressure p: σ = pd/(4t). The stress is the same in all directions (spherical symmetry). Since it is half the hoop stress of a cylinder (pd/2t), a spherical vessel is structurally more efficient."),
    P("What is the volumetric strain of a thin spherical shell under internal pressure?", "ε_v = 3pd(1 – ν)/(4tE).", "Volumetric strain = sum of three equal normal strains.", "In a spherical shell, all three normal strains are equal: ε = pd(1–ν)/(4tE). Volumetric strain ε_v = 3ε = 3pd(1–ν)/(4tE). Change in volume ΔV = V × ε_v = πd⁴p(1–ν)/(8tE)."),
    P("Compare the hoop stress of a thin cylinder and a thin spherical shell of same p, d, t.", "Cylinder: σ_h = pd/(2t). Sphere: σ = pd/(4t). The cylinder stress is twice the sphere stress.", "Spherical vessels are structurally more efficient than cylindrical ones.", "For the same p, d, t, the cylinder has σ_h = pd/(2t) while the sphere has σ = pd/(4t). This is why spherical storage tanks are preferred for high-pressure gas storage and why hemispherical end caps are used on pressure vessels."),
    P("Draw the SFD and BMD for a simply supported beam with central point load W.", "SFD: constant +W/2 from left support to centre, then –W/2 to the right support. BMD: triangular, zero at supports, maximum WL/4 at centre.", "SFD has constant blocks; BMD is triangular.", "For span L with central load W: reactions = W/2 each. SFD: +W/2 from x = 0 to L/2, drops by W at centre, then –W/2 to x = L. BMD: linear from 0 at support to WL/4 at centre, then back to 0. Max BM = WL/4 at midspan."),
    P("Draw the SFD and BMD for a simply supported beam with UDL of intensity w over the full span.", "SFD: linear from +wL/2 at left support to –wL/2 at right support, zero at midspan. BMD: parabolic, zero at supports, maximum wL²/8 at midspan.", "SFD is a straight line; BMD is a second-degree parabola.", "Reactions = wL/2 each. SFD at x: V(x) = wL/2 – wx. BMD at x: M(x) = wLx/2 – wx²/2. Max BM at x = L/2: M_max = wL²/8."),
    P("What is the maximum bending moment for a simply supported beam with a central point load?", "M_max = WL/4 at midspan.", "BM is maximum at the load point.", "For span L with central load W: reactions = W/2 each. BM at centre = (W/2)(L/2) = WL/4. This is the maximum; BM is zero at supports."),
    P("What is the maximum bending moment for a simply supported beam with UDL w over the full span?", "M_max = wL²/8 at midspan.", "For UDL, max BM = wL²/8 — one of the most important formulas.", "BM at x is M(x) = wLx/2 – wx²/2. Setting dM/dx = 0: x = L/2, M_max = wL²/4 – wL²/8 = wL²/8."),
    P("State the relations between load intensity, shear force, and bending moment.", "w = –dV/dx and V = dM/dx. Load intensity equals the negative rate of change of shear force; shear force equals the rate of change of bending moment.", "Differentiation/integration connects w, V, and M.", "From equilibrium of a differential beam element: dV/dx = –w(x) and dM/dx = V(x). For a point load, SF jumps; for UDL, SF is linear; for UVL, SF is parabolic and BM is cubic."),
    P("Draw SFD and BMD for a cantilever with point load W at the free end.", "SFD: constant –W over the full span. BMD: linear, zero at the free end, maximum −WL at the fixed end.", "Cantilever with end load: constant SF, linear hogging BM.", "For cantilever length L with load W at free end: V(x) = –W (constant). M(x) = –Wx (linear), M_max = –WL at the fixed end. BM at the free end = 0."),
    P("Draw SFD and BMD for a cantilever with UDL w over the full span.", "SFD: linear from 0 at free end to –wL at fixed end. BMD: parabolic, 0 at free end, maximum −wL²/2 at the fixed end.", "Cantilever with UDL: linear SF, parabolic BM.", "At distance x from the free end: V(x) = –wx, M(x) = –wx²/2. Maximum values at the fixed end: V_max = –wL, M_max = –wL²/2. All BM is hogging."),
    P("What is the point of contraflexure?", "The point where the bending moment changes sign (from sagging to hogging). At this point BM = 0 while SF is not zero.", "Where the BMD crosses the zero line.", "At the point of contraflexure, the beam curvature changes from concave upward to concave downward. It is where M = 0 in a region of sign change, important in continuous and overhanging beams for deciding reinforcement positions."),
    P("Draw SFD and BMD for a simply supported beam with an eccentric point load W at distance a from the left support.", "SFD: +Wb/L from left to load, then –Wa/L to right end (b = L – a). BMD: triangular, max BM = Wab/L at the load point.", "Reactions: R_A = Wb/L, R_B = Wa/L.", "For load W at distance a from left (b = L – a): SFD has constant +Wb/L up to the load, then –Wa/L. BMD rises linearly to Wab/L at the load point then falls to zero. Max BM = Wab/L."),
    P("What is the maximum BM for a simply supported beam with a UVL varying from 0 at left to w at right?", "M_max = wL²/(9√3) ≈ 0.0642wL² at x = L/√3 from the zero-load end.", "For a triangular load, the position of max BM is at L/√3 from the apex.", "For UVL from 0 at A to w at B: V(x) = wL/6 – wx²/(2L), M(x) = wLx/6 – wx³/(6L). Setting V = 0: x = L/√3. M_max = wL²/(9√3) ≈ 0.0642wL²."),
    P("State the bending equation.", "M/I = σ/y = E/R, where M = bending moment, I = moment of inertia about NA, σ = bending stress at distance y from NA, E = Young's modulus, R = radius of curvature.", "The flexure formula connects moment, stress, and curvature.", "For a beam in pure bending: M/I = σ/y = E/R. So σ = My/I = M/Z where Z = I/y_max is the section modulus. Valid for homogeneous elastic material, plane sections remaining plane, small deformations."),
    P("What is the neutral axis of a beam in bending?", "The neutral axis (NA) is the line in the cross-section where the bending stress is zero. It passes through the centroid of a homogeneous section.", "NA passes through the centroid; zero stress at this line.", "The layer that neither extends nor compresses in bending is the neutral axis. For a homogeneous section, NA passes through the centroid. Bending stress varies linearly from zero at the NA to maximum at the extreme fibres."),
    P("State the assumptions in the theory of simple bending.", "1) Material is homogeneous and isotropic. 2) E is same in tension and compression. 3) Plane sections remain plane. 4) Radius of curvature is large compared to depth. 5) Beam is initially straight. 6) Stresses are within the elastic limit.", "These assumptions validate the linear stress distribution σ = My/I.", "The key assumption is that plane sections remain plane (Bernoulli hypothesis), giving the linear strain distribution. Assumptions of small curvature and elastic behaviour keep the analysis linear. Violations of these require more advanced theories."),
    P("Define section modulus Z.", "Z = I/y_max, where I = moment of inertia about the NA and y_max = distance to the extreme fibre. The maximum bending stress: σ_max = M/Z.", "Z = I/y_max. Higher Z means a stronger section.", "Section modulus combines shape and size into the bending strength factor. Rectangular: Z = bh²/6. Circular: Z = πd³/32. Units are m³ or mm³. Design for strength: Z_req = M/σ_allow."),
    P("What is the section modulus of a rectangular section?", "Z = bh²/6. I = bh³/12, y_max = h/2, so Z = (bh³/12)/(h/2) = bh²/6.", "Z = I/y_max = bh²/6 for rectangles.", "For a rectangle b × h: I_NA = bh³/12, y_max = h/2. Z = bh²/6. For b = 100 mm, h = 200 mm: Z = 100 × 200²/6 = 666,667 mm³."),
    P("What is the section modulus of a solid circular section?", "Z = πd³/32. I = πd⁴/64, y_max = d/2, so Z = (πd⁴/64)/(d/2) = πd³/32.", "For a solid circle: Z = πd³/32.", "For a circle of diameter d: I = πd⁴/64, y_max = d/2, Z = πd³/32. For a hollow circle D/d: Z = π(D⁴ – d⁴)/(32D)."),
    P("What is a composite beam?", "A composite beam is made of two or more materials bonded together to act as a single unit (wood-steel, concrete-steel). The neutral axis does not pass through the overall section centroid.", "Different materials with different E values bonded together.", "In a composite beam, strain is linear across the section (plane sections remain plane) but stress is discontinuous at material interfaces because σ = Eε. The NA shifts toward the stiffer material. Analysis uses the modular ratio n = E₁/E₂."),
    P("How do you find stresses in a composite beam?", "Transform the section into one material using the modular ratio n = E₁/E₂, find the neutral axis and I of the transformed section, then σ = My/I; for the other material multiply by n.", "Use the transformed section method.", "Transform material 1 to material 2 by multiplying its width (or area) by n = E₁/E₂. Find the centroid and I of the transformed section. Stress in material 2 at level y: σ₂ = My/I_tr. Stress in material 1: σ₁ = n × σ₂ at the same level."),
    P("What is a reinforced concrete beam and how is it analysed?", "RCC beam: concrete takes compression, steel takes tension. Analysis uses the transformed section method with modular ratio n = E_s/E_c.", "Concrete is weak in tension; steel reinforcement carries tensile forces.", "Concrete is assumed to take no tension. The NA is found by equating first moments of the transformed steel area (nA_s) and the compression area about the NA. Moment of resistance M = σ_c × I_tr/y_c = σ_s × I_tr/(n(d – y_c)). Modular ratio n = E_s/E_c ≈ 10–15 for working stress design."),
    P("What is the general shear stress formula for beams?", "τ = VQ/(Ib), where V = shear force, Q = first moment of area above the level (Aȳ), I = moment of inertia of the full section, b = width at the level.", "τ = VQ/(Ib) — fundamental for all cross-sections.", "Shear stress at any level y is τ = VQ/(Ib), where Q = ∫y dA over the area above the level. For a rectangular section, this is parabolic. At the NA it is maximum; at extreme fibres it is zero."),
    P("What is the shear stress distribution in a rectangular section?", "Parabolic: τ = (V/(2I))(h²/4 – y²). Maximum at the NA, zero at top and bottom. τ_max = 3V/(2A) = 1.5 × average.", "Parabolic: maximum at the NA, zero at the extreme fibres.", "For a rectangle b × h: shear stress at distance y from the NA is parabolic with maximum at the NA: τ_max = 3V/(2bh) = 1.5 V/A. The extreme fibres carry zero shear stress."),
    P("What is the maximum shear stress in a rectangular section?", "τ_max = 3V/(2A) = 3V/(2bh), occurring at the neutral axis. Average shear stress = V/A.", "Maximum shear stress is 1.5 times the average for rectangles.", "For a rectangular section b × h: τ_max = 3V/(2bh). The factor 1.5 arises because the parabolic distribution peaks at the centroid. This is a standard SSC-JE result."),
    P("What is the shear stress distribution in a circular section?", "τ = (4V/(3A))(1 – y²/R²). Maximum at the centre: τ_max = 4V/(3A) ≈ 1.33 × average.", "Maximum in a solid circular section is 4/3 the average.", "For a solid circle diameter d, area A = πd²/4: τ_max = 4V/(3A) = 16V/(3πd²) at the centre. Distribution is parabolic across the vertical diameter."),
    P("What is the shear stress distribution in an I-section?", "Shear stress is maximum at the NA and nearly uniform in the web. The web carries most of the shear (90–95% for standard I-sections); flanges carry most of the bending moment.", "Web carries the majority of shear; flanges resist bending.", "In an I-section, the web is narrow (small b) so τ = VQ/(Ib) is large and fairly uniform near the NA. τ_max ≈ V/A_web for practical design. The web can be proportioned to resist shear while flanges resist bending."),
    P("What is the double integration method for beam deflection?", "Integrate EI d²y/dx² = M(x) twice: first integration gives slope, second gives deflection. Apply boundary conditions for the constants.", "Governing equation: EI y'' = M(x). Integrate twice, apply BCs.", "From M = EI d²y/dx²: θ = dy/dx = (1/EI)∫M(x)dx + C₁, y = (1/EI)∫∫M(x)dx + C₁x + C₂. Boundary conditions: simply supported ends have y = 0; fixed ends have y = 0 and dy/dx = 0."),
    P("What is Macaulay's method for beam deflection?", "Macaulay's method writes a single moment expression valid over the whole beam using singularity brackets [x – a]ⁿ, which integrate only when the bracket is positive.", "Use Macaulay brackets to handle discontinuities in one equation.", "For a load at x = a, use W[x – a]¹; for UDL starting at a, use w[x – a]²/2. Term-by-term integration with brackets activated only when x > a avoids splitting the beam into segments. Standard values [x – a]ⁿ integrate as [x – a]ⁿ⁺¹/(n+1)."),
    P("State the first moment-area theorem.", "The change in slope between two points A and B equals the area of the M/EI diagram between them: θ_B – θ_A = ∫(M/EI)dx from A to B.", "First theorem: slope change = area of M/EI diagram.", "For an elastic beam, the change in slope between two points equals the area under the M/EI diagram between them. If EI is constant: θ_B – θ_A = (Area of M diagram)/(EI). This is useful for finding slopes in statically determinate beams."),
    P("State the second moment-area theorem.", "The deflection of point B from the tangent at A equals the moment of the M/EI diagram between A and B taken about B: t_B/A = ∫(M/EI)x dx about B.", "Second theorem: tangential deviation = moment of M/EI area about the point.", "t_B/A equals the area of the M/EI diagram times the distance of its centroid from B. Measured perpendicular to the initial position. Combining the two theorems allows computing slopes and deflections of simple beams."),
    P("What is the conjugate beam method?", "The conjugate beam is loaded with the M/EI diagram of the real beam. Slope of the real beam = shear force in the conjugate beam; deflection of the real beam = bending moment in the conjugate beam.", "Replace the M/EI loading on the conjugate beam with proper support conversions.", "Conjugate beam rules: free end (real) → fixed end (conjugate); simple end → simple end; internal hinge → internal support. Load the conjugate beam with M/EI. Then θ(real) = V(conjugate) and δ(real) = M(conjugate)."),
    P("State Castigliano's theorem for deflection.", "The deflection in the direction of a force equals the partial derivative of strain energy with respect to that force: δ = ∂U/∂P. For rotation: θ = ∂U/∂M.", "δ = ∂U/∂P at the point and direction of the force.", "For a structure with strain energy U: deflection δ at the point of application of P in its direction is δ = ∂U/∂P. To find deflection where no load acts, add a fictitious force, differentiate, then set it to zero. Castigliano's theorem uses energy, so it applies to linear elastic structures."),
    P("What are the standard deflection formulas for simply supported beams?", "Central point load W: δ_max = WL³/(48EI). UDL w full span: δ_max = 5wL⁴/(384EI). Eccentric load at a from left: δ = Wa²b²/(3EIL).", "Most commonly used deflection formulas.", "For a simply supported beam: central load δ_max = WL³/(48EI) at centre; full UDL δ_max = 5wL⁴/(384EI) at centre; eccentric load (distances a and b) δ = Wa²b²/(3EIL) at the load point. Slopes at supports: ±WL²/(16EI) and ±wL³/(24EI) respectively."),
    P("What is a propped cantilever?", "A propped cantilever is a cantilever with an additional support at the free end. It is statically indeterminate to the first degree.", "A cantilever with a prop at the free end.", "The propped cantilever has 4 unknown reactions (3 at the fixed end + 1 prop) but only 3 equilibrium equations. The extra condition is zero deflection at the prop. For UDL w: prop reaction = 3wL/8, fixed end moment = wL²/8."),
    P("What is the prop reaction for a propped cantilever with UDL w over the full span?", "R_prop = 3wL/8 upward. Fixed end moment = wL²/8. Max sagging moment = 9wL²/128 at x = 3L/8 from the fixed end.", "Use compatibility: cantilever deflection equals prop deflection.", "Cantilever free-end deflection under UDL = wL⁴/(8EI) downward. Upward prop reaction R gives RL³/(3EI). Equating: R = 3wL/8. Fixed end moment = wL²/2 – 3wL/8 × L = wL²/8. Point of contraflexure at 3L/8 from fixed end."),
    P("What are the fixed end moments for a fixed beam with a central point load?", "M_A = M_B = WL/8 at both ends (hogging). Central sagging moment = WL/8.", "FEMs for central load: WL/8 at each fixed end.", "For a fixed-fixed beam with central load W: reactions = W/2 each. Fixed end moments = WL/8 (hogging). Central moment = WL/4 – WL/8 = WL/8 (sagging). Max deflection = WL³/(192EI) at centre — one quarter of the simply supported value."),
    P("What are the fixed end moments for a fixed beam with UDL w over the full span?", "M_A = M_B = wL²/12 (hogging). Midspan sagging moment = wL²/24. Max deflection = wL⁴/(384EI).", "FEM for UDL: wL²/12 at each end.", "For a fixed-fixed beam with UDL w: FEM = wL²/12 at each support. Max midspan moment (sagging) = wL²/24. Max central deflection = wL⁴/(384EI) — one fifth of the simply supported value. Reactions = wL/2."),
    P("State the Three Moment Theorem (Clapeyron) for continuous beams.", "M_A L₁ + 2M_B(L₁ + L₂) + M_C L₂ = –(6A₁ā₁/L₁ + 6A₂b̄₂/L₂), relating BMs at three consecutive supports A, B, C.", "Clapeyron's theorem relates BMs at three consecutive supports.", "For continuous beams, Clapeyron's Three Moment equation relates the moments at three consecutive supports A, B, C with spans L₁ and L₂. A₁ and A₂ are the areas of the free (simple-support) BMDs on the two spans; ā₁ and b̄₂ measure the centroid positions of those areas."),
    P("Write the Three Moment equation for two equal spans with UDL and simple end supports.", "For equal spans L with UDL w and M_A = M_C = 0: M_B = –wL²/4.", "For equal spans the Three Moment equation simplifies heavily.", "Three Moment: M_A L + 2M_B(2L) + M_C L = –6(wL³/12)/L – 6(wL³/12)/L = –2wL². With M_A = M_C = 0: 4M_B L = –wL³, so M_B = –wL²/4. Reactions: R_A = R_C = 3wL/8, R_B = 5wL/4."),
    P("State the torsion equation.", "T/J = τ/r = Gθ/L. T = torque, J = polar moment of inertia, τ = shear stress at radius r, G = shear modulus, θ = angle of twist, L = length.", "The torsion equation is the circular-shaft counterpart of the bending equation.", "For a circular shaft under torque T: T/J = τ/r = Gθ/L. Max shear stress τ = Tr/J at the outer surface. Angle of twist θ = TL/(GJ). For a solid shaft J = πd⁴/32; hollow J = π(D⁴ – d⁴)/32."),
    P("What is the polar moment of inertia for a solid shaft?", "J = πd⁴/32 = πr⁴/2, where d = diameter.", "For a solid circular section: J = πd⁴/32.", "For a solid circular shaft: J = πd⁴/32. The polar moment is the second moment of area about the axis perpendicular to the section (the shaft axis). Since J = I_x + I_y and I_x = I_y = πd⁴/64, J = πd⁴/32."),
    P("What is the polar moment of inertia for a hollow shaft?", "J = π(D⁴ – d⁴)/32, with D = outer diameter, d = inner diameter. With k = d/D: J = πD⁴(1 – k⁴)/32.", "Subtract the inner circle polar moment from the outer.", "For a hollow shaft: J = πD⁴/32 – πd⁴/32 = π(D⁴ – d⁴)/32. A hollow shaft is more efficient than a solid one of the same material because material near the centre contributes little to J."),
    P("What is the angle of twist for a solid shaft?", "θ = TL/(GJ) = 32TL/(Gπd⁴), radians. T = torque, L = length, G = shear modulus, J = polar moment.", "Angle of twist ∝ T and L, inversely ∝ G and J.", "For a solid shaft diameter d: θ = 32TL/(Gπd⁴). For steel (G = 80 GPa), d = 50 mm, T = 1 kN·m, L = 1 m: θ = 0.0326 rad ≈ 187°... actually 0.0326 rad = 1.87°."),
    P("What is torsional rigidity?", "Torsional rigidity = GJ, where G = shear modulus and J = polar moment of inertia. Units: N·m². It is the torsional analogue of EI for bending.", "GJ is to torsion what EI is to bending.", "Torsional rigidity GJ determines the angle of twist θ = TL/(GJ). Higher GJ means less twist for the same torque. It combines material (G) and section geometry (J). Used in analysis of rotating shafts, machine tools, and torsion bars."),
    P("What is the power transmitted by a shaft?", "P = 2πNT/60 watts, where N = RPM and T = torque in N·m. Equivalently P = Tω with ω = 2πN/60.", "Power = Torque × Angular velocity.", "P = Tω = T × 2πN/60 W. In kW: P = 2πNT/(60,000). In HP: P = 2πNT/(60 × 746). Torque in N·m from kW: T = 9550P/N."),
    P("What is the formula for the diameter of a solid shaft based on strength?", "d³ = 16T/(πτ_allow).", "From τ_max = Tr/J = 16T/(πd³).", "For a solid shaft: τ_max = T(d/2)/(πd⁴/32) = 16T/(πd³). Therefore d = ∛(16T/(πτ_allow)). For hollow shafts: d_o³ = 16T/(πτ_allow(1 – k⁴))."),
    P("What happens when two shafts are connected in series?", "Same torque in both shafts. Total twist θ = θ₁ + θ₂ = TL/(G₁J₁) + TL/(G₂J₂). Equivalent twist stiffness behaves like springs in series.", "Series: torque same, twists add.", "For shafts in series, the torque through both is identical (no branch). Total angle of twist adds: θ_total = T(L₁/(G₁J₁) + L₂/(G₂J₂)). The equivalent GJ satisfies 1/GJ_eq = 1/(G₁J₁) + 1/(G₂J₂)."),
    P("What happens when two shafts are connected in parallel?", "Same twist in both shafts. Total torque T = T₁ + T₂ = (G₁J₁ + G₂J₂)θ/L. Equivalent stiffness GJ_eq = G₁J₁ + G₂J₂.", "Parallel: same angle of twist, torques add.", "For parallel shafts connected to the same end members, both twist by the same angle θ. Torque splits inversely to the twist stiffnesses. GJ_eq = G₁J₁ + G₂J₂, analogous to springs in parallel."),
    P("What is the deflection of a close-coiled helical spring under axial load?", "δ = 8WD³n/(Gd⁴), where W = load, D = mean coil diameter, d = wire diameter, n = number of active coils, G = shear modulus.", "Helical spring formula: δ = 8WD³n/(Gd⁴).", "Under axial load W, the spring wire is twisted. The axial deflection is δ = 8WD³n/(Gd⁴). The spring stiffness k = W/δ = Gd⁴/(8D³n)."),
    P("What is the stiffness (rate) of a helical spring?", "k = W/δ = Gd⁴/(8D³n). Stiffer when d is larger, D and n are smaller.", "Spring rate k = Gd⁴/(8D³n).", "The spring constant k = Gd⁴/(8D³n). Doubling the wire diameter increases stiffness 16 times. Doubling the mean coil diameter reduces stiffness 8 times. Increasing the number of coils reduces stiffness proportionally."),
    P("What is the deflection of a cantilever leaf spring?", "δ = 6Wl³/(Ebt³n), where l = leaf length, b = width, t = thickness, n = number of leaves.", "Leaf spring (cantilever type): δ = 6Wl³/(Ebt³n).", "A cantilever leaf spring with n leaves of width b, thickness t, length l under load W deflects by δ = 6Wl³/(Ebt³n). Each leaf bends as a cantilever of length l; sharing among the n leaves divides the load."),
    P("What is the maximum shear stress in a close-coiled helical spring (without Wahl factor)?", "τ = 8WD/(πd³) + 4W/(πd²). The second term is small; commonly τ = 8WD/(πd³).", "Combine direct shear with torsional shear.", "In a close-coiled helical spring, the wire carries a torque WD/2 and a direct shear W. τ = 16T/(πd³) + 4W/(πd²) = 8WD/(πd³) + 4W/(πd²). The direct shear term is usually neglected: τ ≈ 8WD/(πd³)."),
    P("What is the Wahl correction factor in helical spring design?", "Wahl factor K_w = (4C – 1)/(4C – 4) + 0.615/C, where C = D/d is the spring index. It accounts for stress concentration due to curvature and direct shear.", "Wahl factor corrects the nominal torsional stress for curvature effects.", "The Wahl factor combines the effects of shear stress concentration and direct shear. Actual max stress τ = K_w × 8WD/(πd³). For C = 6: K_w ≈ 1.25. For larger spring indices, K_w approaches 1."),
    P("What is the spring index C and its standard range?", "Spring index C = D/d (mean coil diameter / wire diameter). Practical range is 4 to 12.", "C = D/d; values between 4 and 12 are common.", "Spring index C = D/d. A low C gives a stiff, strong spring but more stress concentration. High C gives flexible springs but risk of buckling and tangling. C = 4–12 is the recommended design range."),
    P("How do you combine helical springs in series?", "1/k_eq = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂, where k = Gd⁴/(8D³n) for each. Equal load W; deflections add.", "Springs in series: deflections add, loads equal.", "For springs in series, the same load passes through both springs and the total deflection is the sum. The equivalent stiffness: 1/k_eq = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂, so k_eq = k₁k₂/(k₁ + k₂), which is smaller than either spring."),
    P("How do you combine helical springs in parallel?", "k_eq = k₁ + k₂. Equal deflection; loads split in proportion to stiffness.", "Springs in parallel: loads add, deflections equal.", "Springs in parallel share the load in proportion to their stiffnesses: k_eq = k₁ + k₂. Each spring deflects by the same amount. The stiffer spring carries more load. This arrangement is used in valve springs of IC engines."),
    P("What is Euler's buckling load for long columns?", "P_cr = π²EI/(Le²), where E = Young's modulus, I = least moment of inertia, Le = effective length.", "Euler's formula: P_cr = π²EI/Le².", "Euler's critical load is the load at which a long column buckles elastically: P_cr = π²EI/(Le²). It is valid only when the critical stress π²E/(Le/k)² is below the proportional limit; otherwise Rankine's formula is used."),
    P("What is the effective length of a column for the four standard end conditions?", "Pinned-pinned: L_e = L. Fixed-fixed: L_e = L/2. Fixed-free: L_e = 2L. Fixed-pinned: L_e = L/√2 ≈ 0.7L.", "Effective length depends on end fixity.", "End conditions: both pinned: L_e = L; both fixed: L_e = L/2; fixed and free: L_e = 2L; fixed and pinned: L_e = 0.7L. The corresponding Euler loads: π²EI/L², 4π²EI/L², π²EI/4L², and ≈2π²EI/L²."),
    P("Define slenderness ratio.", "Slenderness ratio λ = L_e/k, where L_e = effective length and k = radius of gyration = √(I/A).", "λ = L_e/k.", "The slenderness ratio measures the tendency to buckle. For a circular section, k = d/4; for a rectangle b × h, k_min = b/√12 (about the weak axis). A high slenderness ratio indicates slender, buckling-prone columns."),
    P("What is Rankine's formula for columns?", "P_R = σ_cA/(1 + a(L_e/k)²), with a = σ_c/(π²E). It combines crushing and elastic buckling resistances.", "Rankine: P_R = σ_cA/(1 + a(L_e/k)²).", "Rankine's formula interpolates between short-column crushing and long-column buckling: 1/P_R = 1/P_c + 1/P_E. For steel, a ≈ 1/7500 with σ_c ≈ 330 MPa and E = 200 GPa. Standard values of a are given in codes."),
    P("What is the critical (limiting) slenderness ratio?", "The value of L_e/k above which Euler's formula is valid and below which failure is by crushing. For mild steel, it is approximately 100.", "Euler validity limit: for steel L_e/k ≈ 100.", "Euler's formula is valid if the Euler critical stress σ_cr = π²E/(L_e/k)² is below the proportional limit. For steel with E = 200 GPa and proportional limit ≈ 200 MPa: L_e/k > √(π²×200×10⁹/200×10⁶) ≈ 99.3. Below this, crushing governs."),
    P("What is Euler's buckling stress for a column?", "σ_cr = π²E/(L_e/k)² = π²EA/(L_e²/A)… explicitly σ_cr = π²E/λ², with λ = L_e/k.", "Euler critical stress = π²E/λ².", "Euler's buckling stress is σ_cr = P_cr/A = π²EI/(A L_e²) = π²E/(L_e/k)² = π²E/λ². The stress depends only on E and the slenderness ratio, not the material strength. High slenderness means very low buckling stress."),
    P("What is the radius of gyration of a section?", "k = √(I/A), where I = moment of inertia and A = cross-sectional area. For buckling, the least value of k is used.", "k = √(I/A).", "The radius of gyration is the distance at which the entire area could be concentrated to give the same moment of inertia. For a solid circle: k = d/4; for a rectangle b × h: k = b/√12 (smaller about the narrow dimension). Columns buckle about the axis with minimum k."),
    P("What is the effect of end conditions on the Euler buckling load?", "Fixed-fixed is strongest (P_cr = 4π²EI/L²), then fixed-pinned (≈2π²EI/L²), then pinned-pinned (π²EI/L²), then fixed-free (π²EI/4L²).", "More end restraint means a higher buckling load.", "Euler load P_cr = π²EI/(L_e²). Effective lengths: fixed-fixed L/2, fixed-pinned 0.7L, pinned-pinned L, fixed-free 2L. The strength factors relative to pinned-pinned: 4, ≈2, 1, and 0.25 respectively."),
    P("What is eccentric loading on a short column?", "σ = P/A ± Pey/I. Maximum compressive stress = P/A + Pey_max/I = (P/A)(1 + Ae·y_max/I).", "Eccentric load produces combined direct and bending compression.", "For load P at eccentricity e: the direct stress is P/A and bending stress is ±Pey/I. The maximum stress: σ_max = P/A + Pey_max/I. No tension develops if e is small enough that P/A ≥ Pey_max/I, defining the core of the section."),
    P("What is the core (kern) of a rectangular section?", "The core of a rectangle b × h is a rhombus with diagonals b/3 and h/3. The load must lie within it to avoid tension.", "For a rectangle the kern is a rhombus with semi-diagonals b/6 and h/6.", "For a rectangular section, the kern (middle third rule) ensures zero tension: eccentricity along depth must be e ≤ h/6 and along width e ≤ b/6. The kern is a rhombus of area bh/18 with diagonals b/3 and h/3. Masonry and concrete columns are designed so the resultant load stays in the core."),
    P("What is the core (kern) of a circular section?", "The core of a solid circular section is a concentric circle of diameter d/4. The load must lie within it to avoid tension.", "For a circular section the kern is a circle of diameter d/4.", "For a circular column, the kern is a concentric circle of radius e = d/8 (diameter d/4). This comes from setting P/A = Pey/I with y = d/2: e = I/(Ay) = (πd⁴/64)/(πd²/4 × d/2) = d/8. The middle-fourth rule applies for circles."),
    P("State Rankine's theory (maximum principal stress theory) of failure.", "Failure occurs when the maximum principal stress reaches the elastic limit in simple tension: σ₁ = σ_el. Suitable for brittle materials such as cast iron.", "Rankine criterion: σ₁ = σ_el.", "Rankine's theory is used for brittle materials that fracture on the plane of maximum tension. For cast iron, concrete, and glass it matches experiments better than other theories. For ductile materials it is unsafe because it ignores shear effects."),
    P("State the maximum shear stress theory (Tresca) of failure.", "Failure occurs when the maximum shear stress equals the shear stress at yield in simple tension: τ_max = σ_y/2, or σ₁ – σ₃ = σ_y. Suitable for ductile materials.", "Tresca criterion: σ₁ – σ₃ = σ_y.", "The Tresca theory states that yielding begins when τ_max = (σ₁ – σ₃)/2 reaches the yield shear stress σ_y/2. It is simple, conservative, and correct for ductile materials under low hydrostatic stress. Widely used in pressure-vessel and machine design codes."),
    P("State the maximum principal strain theory (St. Venant) of failure.", "Failure occurs when the maximum principal strain equals the yield strain: ε₁ = σ_y/E, or σ₁ – ν(σ₂ + σ₃) = σ_y.", "St. Venant: σ₁ – ν(σ₂ + σ₃) = σ_y.", "The maximum strain theory predicts failure when ε₁ = σ_y/E. For biaxial stress: σ₁ – νσ₂ = σ_y. It was used historically but agrees poorly with experimental data for ductile materials and slightly overpredicts strength. Rarely used in modern design."),
    P("State the total strain energy theory (Beltrami-Haigh) of failure.", "Failure occurs when the total strain energy per unit volume equals the strain energy at yield in simple tension: σ₁² + σ₂² – 2νσ₁σ₂ = σ_y².", "Beltrami-Haigh: u = σ_y²/(2E).", "Beltrami's theory sets the total strain energy density (U = σ_y²/(2E) in uniaxial tension) equal to the yield value. For biaxial stress: σ₁² + σ₂² – 2νσ₁σ₂ = σ_y². It ignores the distinction between volumetric and distortion energy, so design predictions are not accurate for ductile materials."),
    P("State the maximum distortion energy theory (von Mises) of failure.", "Failure occurs when the distortion (shear) strain energy per unit volume reaches the yield value. Criterion: (σ₁ – σ₂)² + (σ₂ – σ₃)² + (σ₃ – σ₁)² = 2σ_y².", "Von Mises criterion: σ_vm = σ_y.", "The von Mises (maximum distortion energy) theory is the most accurate for ductile materials. For biaxial stress: σ₁² – σ₁σ₂ + σ₂² = σ_y². The von Mises stress σ_vm = √(σ₁² – σ₁σ₂ + σ₂²). Experiments confirm this criterion holds best for mild steel and aluminium."),
    P("Which theories of failure suit ductile and which suit brittle materials?", "Ductile materials: maximum shear stress (Tresca) or maximum distortion energy (von Mises). Brittle materials: maximum principal stress (Rankine).", "Tresca/von Mises for ductile, Rankine for brittle.", "Ductile materials fail by yielding (slip), driven by shear stress, so Tresca (conservative) and von Mises (accurate) are used. Brittle materials (cast iron, concrete, ceramics) fail by fracture under tension, so the maximum principal stress theory applies. The maximum strain and total strain energy theories are largely obsolete."),
    P("What is the equivalent bending moment for combined bending and torsion?", "M_e = ½[M + √(M² + T²)]. It gives the same maximum normal stress as the combined loading.", "M_e = ½[M + √(M² + T²)].", "The equivalent bending moment M_e = ½[M + √(M² + T²)] produces the same extreme fibre stress as the combined bending moment M and torque T. Maximum normal stress σ_max = 32M_e/(πd³). Used to design shafts subjected to both bending and torsion."),
    P("What is the equivalent twisting moment for combined bending and torsion?", "T_e = √(M² + T²). It gives the same maximum shear stress as the combined loading.", "T_e = √(M² + T²).", "The equivalent twisting moment T_e = √(M² + T²) produces the same maximum shear stress as the actual combined loading. Maximum shear stress τ_max = 16T_e/(πd³). Design of a shaft for shear: d³ = 16T_e/(πτ_allow)."),
    P("What is the maximum principal stress in a shaft under combined bending and torsion?", "σ₁ = 16/(πd³) × [M + √(M² + T²)] = 32M_e/(πd³).", "σ₁ = 16/(πd³)[M + √(M² + T²)].", "For a solid shaft under bending M and torque T: the maximum normal stress is σ₁ = (16/(πd³))[M + √(M² + T²)], and the minimum is σ₂ = (16/(πd³))[M – √(M² + T²)]. Maximum shear τ_max = 16√(M² + T²)/(πd³) = T_e×16/(πd³)."),
    P("Define impact loading.", "Impact loading occurs when a load is applied suddenly or with velocity, producing dynamic stresses greater than the static equivalents.", "Impact loading introduces dynamic amplification of stress.", "When a weight falls onto a member, the kinetic energy is converted into strain energy, producing dynamic stress greater than the static stress. This is quantified by the impact factor: dynamic stress = impact factor × static stress. Impact loading is critical in crane lines, drop hammers, and vehicle collisions."),
    P("What is the impact factor formula?", "Impact factor = 1 + √(1 + 2h/δ_st), where h = fall height and δ_st = static deflection under the load.", "Impact factor amplifies the static deflection and stress.", "When a weight W falls from height h onto a member with static deflection δ_st: energy conservation gives W(h + δ_d) = ½kδ_d². Solving: δ_d = δ_st[1 + √(1 + 2h/δ_st)]. Dynamic stress and deflection are the impact factor times static values. For h = 0 the factor is 2."),
    P("What is the impact stress in a bar when a weight falls from a height?", "σ_d = σ_st × (1 + √(1 + 2hAE/(WL))), where σ_st = W/A, h = fall height, L = bar length, A = area, E = elastic modulus.", "Dynamic stress = impact factor × static stress.", "For a vertical bar loaded by a weight W falling from height h: static deflection δ_st = WL/(AE), static stress σ_st = W/A. Impact stress σ_d = (W/A)[1 + √(1 + 2hAE/(WL))]. As the fall height increases, dynamic stress grows roughly as √h."),
    P("How does a suddenly applied load differ from a gradually applied load?", "A gradually applied load produces static stress and deflection. A suddenly applied load (h = 0) doubles them: dynamic factor = 2.", "Sudden load: factor 2; gradual load: factor 1.", "For a gradually applied load, the load builds slowly so the member is always in quasi-static equilibrium: dynamic factor = 1. A suddenly applied load (as if dropped from zero height) gives impact factor = 1 + √(1 + 0) = 2. So a suddenly applied load produces twice the stress and twice the deflection."),
    P("What is the strain energy absorbed during impact loading?", "The work done by the falling weight equals the strain energy: W(h + δ_d) = U, with U = σ²_d AL/(2E) for a bar.", "Energy conservation links impact stress to strain energy.", "For a weight W falling from height h onto a bar: U = W(h + δ_d). For a bar of length L and area A: U = σ²_dAL/(2E). Equating: σ²_dAL/(2E) = W(h + WL/(AE)). Solving gives the dynamic stress formula and shows how absorbed energy grows with fall height."),
    P("What is the deflection of a cantilever with a point load at the free end?", "δ_max = WL³/(3EI) at the free end. Slope at the free end θ = WL²/(2EI).", "Cantilever end load: δ = WL³/(3EI).", "For a cantilever of length L with end load W: the deflection at the free end = WL³/(3EI) and the slope = WL²/(2EI). The maximum BM is at the fixed end (WL, hogging). These are first principles using double integration."),
    P("What is the deflection of a cantilever with UDL over the full span?", "δ_max = wL⁴/(8EI) at the free end. Slope at free end = wL³/(6EI).", "Cantilever UDL: δ = wL⁴/(8EI).", "For a cantilever under uniform load w: the free-end deflection = wL⁴/(8EI) and the free-end slope = wL³/(6EI). Maximum BM = wL²/2 at the fixed end. The deflection is bigger than for an equal-end point load because the load acts over the entire length. "),
    P("What is the maximum deflection of a simply supported beam with an eccentric point load?", "δ at the load point = Wa²b²/(3EIL), with a and b the distances from supports. Maximum deflection is slightly off the load point toward the nearer support.", "Eccentric load: δ = Wa²b²/(3EIL) at the load.", "For a simply supported beam spanning L with load W at distance a from A (b = L – a): the deflection at the load point is Wa²b²/(3EIL). It is maximum when the load is central: WL³/(48EI). The truly maximum deflection occurs at √((L² – b²)/3) from A."),
    P("What is the slope at the supports of a simply supported beam with central point load?", "θ_A = –θ_B = WL²/(16EI).", "Slope at supports for central load = WL²/16EI.", "For a simply supported beam with central load W: by symmetry the slopes at the two supports are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction: θ = WL²/(16EI) (using the moment-area or double integration method)."),
    P("What is the slope at the supports of a simply supported beam with UDL?", "θ_A = –θ_B = wL³/(24EI).", "Slope at supports for UDL = wL³/24EI.", "For a simply supported beam with full UDL w: the slope at each support is wL³/(24EI), equal in magnitude, opposite in sign, due to symmetry. The midspan deflection is 5wL⁴/(384EI)."),
    P("What is the volumetric strain of a thin cylindrical shell under internal pressure?", "ε_v = pd(5 – 4ν)/(4tE). For steel (ν = 0.3): ε_v = 0.95pd/(tE).", "Combine hoop and longitudinal strains: ε_v = 2ε_h + ε_L.", "For a thin cylinder: hoop strain ε_h = pd(2 – ν)/(4tE), longitudinal strain ε_L = pd(1 – 2ν)/(4tE). The volumetric strain (capacity change) = 2ε_h + ε_L = pd(5 – 4ν)/(4tE). For ν = 0.3: ε_v = 3.8pd/(4tE) = 0.95pd/(tE)."),
    P("What is the change in diameter of a thin cylinder under internal pressure?", "Δd = d×ε_h = pd²(2 – ν)/(4tE).", "Diameter change = diameter × hoop strain.", "The hoop strain is ε_h = (σ_h – νσ_L)/E = pd(2 – ν)/(4tE). The change in diameter Δd = d×ε_h = pd²(2 – ν)/(4tE). For ν = 0.3: Δd = 1.7pd²/(4tE) = 0.425pd²/(tE)."),
    P("When is a cylinder considered thin-walled?", "When the thickness t is about 1/20 or less of the internal diameter d (d/t ≥ 20, i.e., t/d ≤ 1/20).", "Rule of thumb: d/t ≥ 20 → thin cylinder.", "A cylinder is thin-walled when t/d ≤ 1/20. Then stresses are assumed uniform across the wall and the simple formulas σ_h = pd/(2t), σ_L = pd/(4t) apply. For thicker walls (d/t < 20), Lame's equations must be used because stress varies across the wall."),
    P("What is the bursting pressure of a thin cylinder or sphere?", "Cylinder bursting pressure from hoop stress: p = 2tσ_allow/d. Sphere: p = 4tσ_allow/d. The sphere bursts at twice the pressure of a cylinder of the same size and stress.", "Sphere bursts at 2× the pressure of a cylinder (same t, σ).", "For a thin cylinder, p = 2tσ_allow/d based on the hoop stress. For a thin sphere, p = 4tσ_allow/d. Since the sphere stress is pd/4t (half of the cylinder hoop stress), the sphere sustains twice the internal pressure before bursting."),
    P("What is a shear force and a bending moment at a beam section?", "Shear force is the algebraic sum of all vertical forces on one side of the section. Bending moment is the algebraic sum of the moments of all forces on one side of the section about the section.", "Define from statics on the free body to either side.", "At any section, the shear force V equals the sum of the transverse forces on one side, and the bending moment M equals the sum of their moments about the section. These internal resultants keep the cut part of the beam in equilibrium."),
    P("What is the maximum bending stress in a beam and where does it occur?", "σ_max = M/Z at the extreme fibres farthest from the neutral axis. It is tensile on one face and compressive on the other.", "σ_max = M/Z at the extreme fibres.", "The bending stress at distance y from the NA is σ = My/I. At the extreme fibres σ_max = My_max/I = M/Z. For a sagging beam the top is compressed and the bottom is under tension."),
    P("What is the relation between flexural rigidity, slope, and moment for a beam?", "M = EI d²y/dx². The slope is θ = dy/dx and the curvature is 1/R = d²y/dx². EI is the flexural rigidity.", "Moment = flexural rigidity × curvature.", "For small deflections: 1/R = d²y/dx² ≈ M/EI, so M = EI(d²y/dx²). The slope θ = dy/dx, and the rotation at any point is obtained by one integration; the deflection by a second integration. EI quantifies the beam's resistance to bending."),
    P("What is the difference between resilience and toughness?", "Resilience is the strain energy up to the elastic limit (σ²/2E); toughness is the total energy up to fracture (full area under the σ–ε curve).", "Resilience = elastic energy; toughness = energy to fracture.", "Resilience measures the energy a material can absorb elastically and return; toughness measures the energy absorbed until failure. A spring steel has high resilience; mild steel has high toughness. Soft rubber absorbs energy by large deformation relatively reversibly, while hard materials may be brittle."),
    P("What is a rigid joint and a pin joint in structural frames?", "A rigid (fixed) joint allows no relative rotation between connected members; a pin (hinged) joint allows free rotation and transmits only forces, not moments.", "Pin joints transmit forces only; rigid joints carry moments.", "In a pinned joint, members can rotate freely, so the joint transmits axial and shear forces but near-zero bending moment. In a rigid joint, the members are welded or monolithic, so the joint transmits bending moments and resists relative rotation, increasing frame stiffness."),
    P("What are the typical values of Young's modulus E for common materials?", "Steel ≈ 200 GPa, cast iron ≈ 100–170 GPa, brass ≈ 100 GPa, copper ≈ 120 GPa, aluminium ≈ 70 GPa, concrete ≈ 14–30 GPa, rubber ≈ 0.01 GPa.", "Recall the relative stiffness ordering of materials.", "E measures stiffness: steel ≈ 200 GPa is the reference. Cast iron has E ≈ 125 GPa average, brass ≈ 100 GPa, copper ≈ 120 GPa, aluminium ≈ 70 GPa (about one third of steel), concrete ≈ 25 GPa, and rubber is extremely low, ≈ 10 MPa. These values are used in modular ratio computations."),
    P("What is the formula for combined direct and bending stress (column with eccentric vertical load)?", "σ = P/A ± M·y/I = P/A ± (P·e·y)/I. The maximum compressive stress = P/A + Pe·y_max/I and the minimum = P/A – Pe·y_max/I.", "Eccentric vertical load creates a bending stress added to the direct stress.", "For a short member carrying axial load P at eccentricity e: the stress at any fibre = P/A + (P·e·y)/I. Tension appears if P·e·y_max/I > P/A. This basis leads to 'no-tension' conditions and defines the core (kern) of the section. For a chimney with wind, no tension requires the resultant to fall within the middle third."),
    ];
SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM.tom = [
    P("What is a mechanism?", "A mechanism is a kinematic chain with one link fixed (frame). It converts motion of one form into another with constrained relative motion between links.", "A kinematic chain with a fixed link becomes a mechanism.", "A mechanism is an assembly of rigid links connected by kinematic pairs, with one link fixed as the frame. Degrees of freedom DOF = 3(n–1) – 2j₁ – j₂. A four-bar linkage with 4 links and 4 turning pairs has DOF = 3(3) – 2(4) = 1."),
    P("What is a kinematic pair?", "A kinematic pair is a joint between two links that allows relative motion while constraining some degrees of freedom.", "A joint constrains relative motion between two links.", "A kinematic pair connects two links so they can move in a specific way. Classification: by connection (lower pair: surface contact like turning/sliding; higher pair: point/line contact like cam and follower), by motion (sliding, turning, rolling, screw, spherical), and by closure (self-closed/form-closed or force-closed)."),
    P("State the Grübler (Kutzbach) criterion for planar mechanisms.", "DOF = 3(n – 1) – 2j₁ – j₂, where n = number of links, j₁ = number of lower pairs (1 DOF joints), j₂ = number of higher pairs (2 DOF joints).", "Grübler / Kutzbach: DOF = 3(n–1) – 2j₁ – j₂.", "For a planar mechanism, Kutzbach's criterion gives the number of degrees of freedom. For a four-bar: n = 4, j₁ = 4, j₂ = 0 → DOF = 3(3) – 8 = 1. For a mechanism with 5 links and 5 turning pairs: DOF = 12 – 10 = 2."),
    P("What is a four-bar linkage?", "A four-bar linkage has four links connected in a loop by four revolute joints: the frame, crank, coupler, and follower. It has one DOF.", "Simplest closed mechanism: 4 links, 4 turning pairs.", "In a four-bar mechanism the fixed link is the frame. If the shortest link rotates a full 360°, it is called the crank; the coupler transmits; the follower oscillates. The velocity ratio varies during the motion, making four-bar chains suitable for many machine mechanisms."),
    P("State Grashof's law for four-bar linkages.", "If the sum of the shortest and the longest link lengths is less than or equal to the sum of the other two (s + l ≤ p + q), at least one link can make a complete rotation.", "Grashof condition: s + l ≤ p + q.", "If s + l ≤ p + q, it is a Grashof mechanism: (1) if the shortest link is the frame → double-crank; (2) if the shortest is adjacent to the frame → crank-rocker; (3) if the shortest is the coupler → double-rocker. If s + l > p + q, no link can rotate fully and the mechanism is a double-rocker."),
    P("What is a crank and rocker mechanism?", "A crank-rocker is a Grashof four-bar where the shortest link (crank) rotates fully while the follower (rocker) oscillates without full rotation.", "Crank rotates fully; rocker oscillates.", "When the shortest link is adjacent to the fixed link and the Grashof condition holds, the shortest link becomes a crank (full rotation) and the opposite link oscillates as a rocker. This mechanism is used in engine valve systems, steering linkages, and industrial machines where continuous rotation creates an oscillating output."),
    P("What is a slider-crank mechanism?", "A slider-crank converts rotary motion of the crank into reciprocating motion of the slider (or vice versa). It has a frame, crank, connecting rod, and slider with one sliding pair.", "Crank rotates; slider reciprocates along a straight guide.", "In the slider-crank mechanism, the crank pin moves in a circle; the connecting rod transmits motion to the slider which reciprocates in a fixed straight guide. Stroke = 2 × crank radius. Used in reciprocating engines, compressors, and pumps. At the dead centres the slider velocity is zero."),
    P("What is stroke length in a slider-crank mechanism?", "Stroke = 2 × crank radius (r). The slider travels between the two dead positions (top and bottom dead centre).", "Stroke = 2r, twice the crank radius.", "The slider moves between extreme positions at the dead centres, where crank and connecting rod are collinear. The stroke (piston travel) equals the diameter of the crank circle = 2r. For a given bore, the stroke determines the swept volume of the cylinder."),
    P("What is a double slider-crank mechanism?", "A double slider-crank has two sliding pairs and two turning pairs. Examples are the Oldham coupling (parallel shafts with offset) and the Scotch yoke (simple harmonic motion).", "Four links with two sliding and two turning pairs.", "The double slider-crank has 4 links with 2 sliding pairs and 2 turning pairs. The Oldham coupling connects two parallel shafts with a small radial offset. The Scotch yoke converts uniform rotation into simple harmonic reciprocal motion without a connecting rod."),
    P("What is a quick-return mechanism?", "A quick-return mechanism gives a slow forward (cutting) stroke and a fast return (idle) stroke, saving time in shaping machines.", "Cutting stroke slow for quality; return stroke fast to save time.", "In shaping machines the return stroke does no cutting and is made faster. The driving crank sweeps a larger angle for the cutting stroke and a smaller angle for the return. Quick-return ratio QRR = cutting time / return time = (360° – α)/α, where α is the crank angle of the return stroke."),
    P("What is the Whitworth quick-return mechanism?", "In the Whitworth mechanism the driving crank rotates uniformly while a slotted lever oscillates; the crank pin slides in the slot, producing a faster return stroke than cutting stroke.", "Uniformly rotating crank, oscillating slotted lever, sliding block.", "The Whitworth is an inversion of the double slider-crank: the crank pin slides inside the slot of a lever that oscillates about a fixed pivot. The lever rotates slowly during the working stroke and quickly on return, giving the quick-return action. Used in shaping machines and rotary engines."),
    P("What is the Geneva mechanism?", "The Geneva (Maltese cross) mechanism converts continuous rotation into intermittent rotary motion, rotating the driven wheel in equal steps.", "A pin on the driver engages slots of the Geneva wheel intermittently.", "A pin on the continuously rotating driving wheel enters a slot of the Geneva cross and turns it by a step (e.g., 90° for a four-slot wheel), then leaves as a dwell arc holds the wheel stationary. Used in film projectors, indexing tables, and clock movements."),
    P("What is a toggle mechanism?", "A toggle mechanism amplifies a small input force into a large output force near a dead-centre position, where the links become nearly collinear. Mechanical advantage becomes very large near dead centre.", "Links nearly collinear give huge mechanical advantage.", "In a toggle mechanism (stone crusher, hand press), the input moves the toggle links near the collinear position and the output force rises very steeply as dead centre is approached. At the exact dead-centre position the output force is theoretically infinite, so the mechanism holds position without an extra lock."),
    P("What is a kinematic chain?", "A kinematic chain is an assembly of links and kinematic pairs such that the relative motion of all links is completely constrained.", "A chain where every link has constrained relative motion with its neighbours.", "A kinematic chain connects links through pairs so relative motion is definite. If one link is fixed it becomes a mechanism; if it has zero DOF it becomes a structure. The DOF = 3(n–1) – 2j₁ – j₂ determines the nature: DOF > 0 → mechanism, DOF = 0 → structure."),
    P("What is a machine and how does it differ from a mechanism?", "A machine transmits and transforms power to do useful work, while a mechanism only transmits motion.", "Mechanism transmits motion; machine transforms and transmits power.", "A mechanism is a motion-transmitting device, whereas a machine consists of one or more mechanisms that receive energy and transmit power to do useful work. The same mechanism can be a part of different machines. A kinematic chain converts the constrained motion of links into a prescribed output motion when power is applied."),
    P("What are the inversions of a four-bar chain?", "The three inversions are: (1) beam engine (crank-rocker with coupler), (2) coupled wheels of locomotive (double crank), and (3) double-rocker mechanisms such as the drag link and pantograph.", "Fixing a different link of the four-bar gives a different mechanism.", "By fixing different links of the four-bar chain we obtain the crank-rocker, the double-crank, and the double-rocker mechanisms. Practical inversions include the coupled wheels of a locomotive (double crank), the beam engine linkage, and the drag-link mechanism, each fixing a different link in the chain."),
    P("What is the coupled wheels (double crank) inversion of a four-bar?", "Fixing the coupler makes both cranks rotate fully (double crank); the coupled wheels of a locomotive use this so both driving wheels turn at the same speed and direction.", "Double crank: shortest and other side links both rotate fully.", "In the locomotive coupling, both wheels are cranks connected by a coupler link; the frame is the coupler side. Both wheels thus rotate together at the same speed and in the same phase. This double-crank inversion ensures synchronized wheel motion over both rails."),
    P("What is an oscillating cylinder mechanism?", "Fixing the connecting rod in the slider-crank chain and letting the cylinder oscillate about a pivot gives the oscillating-cylinder mechanism, used in some rotary and marine engines.", "Fix the connecting rod; the cylinder oscillates about its pivot.", "In this inversion the connecting rod is fixed, and the cylinder becomes a guide that oscillates about a pivot while the crank rotates. It is useful in small engines where the whole cylinder may swing to follow the crank and in certain steam- and gas-engine drives."),
    P("What is an instantaneous centre (I-centre)?", "The instantaneous centre of rotation of a body is the point about which the body has pure rotation at that instant; its velocity is zero at that instant.", "The point with zero velocity about which the body rotates momentarily.", "For a body in plane motion, there is always a point with zero instantaneous velocity, the I-centre. Velocities of all points are v = ω × (distance from the I-centre). For pure translation the I-centre lies at infinity. The common normal construction locates I-centres for relative motion."),
    P("State Kennedy's theorem of three instantaneous centres.", "For three bodies in relative plane motion, the three instantaneous centres of the three pairs (I₁₂, I₂₃, I₃₁) lie on a straight line.", "Three I-centres of three moving bodies are always collinear.", "Kennedy's theorem helps locate unknown instantaneous centres. For n links the total number of I-centres = n(n–1)/2. To find an unknown centre, combine two triads of links and take the intersection of the two straight lines from Kennedy's theorem."),
    P("How many instantaneous centres are in a four-bar mechanism?", "For n = 4 links, n(n–1)/2 = 6 instantaneous centres. Four coincide with the turning pairs; the other two (I₁₃, I₂₄) come from Kennedy's theorem.", "Number of I-centres = n(n–1)/2; a four-bar has 6.", "In a four-bar chain: I₁₂, I₂₃, I₃₄, I₄₁ coincide with the four turning-pair centres. I₁₃ is at the intersection of lines I₁₂I₂₃ and I₁₄I₄₃, and I₂₄ at the intersection of I₂₁I₁₄ and I₂₃I₃₄. These centres give velocities directly from the input angular velocity."),
    P("What is the velocity of a point on a rolling wheel? (rolling without slip)", "For a wheel of radius R rolling without slip with centre velocity v: the contact point is the I-centre (zero velocity), the centre moves at v = ωR, and the top point has velocity 2v.", "Contact point = I-centre (zero); top of wheel = 2v.", "When a wheel rolls without slipping, the instantaneous centre is the contact point with the ground. Centre velocity v = ωR. Any point has velocity ω × distance from the contact point; the top point, at distance 2R, has 2ωR = 2v."),
    P("Explain the relative velocity method for mechanism analysis.", "In a mechanism, v_B = v_A + v_BA, where v_BA = ω_AB × r_BA is perpendicular to AB. Drawing these vector equations to scale yields a velocity polygon.", "Relative velocity of B with respect to A = ω×AB, perpendicular to the link.", "Starting from a point of known velocity (usually the fixed frame = zero), the velocity polygon is built using v_B = v_A + v_BA for each link. Since velocity vectors are perpendicular to their links, the closure of the polygon yields velocities of all points and the angular velocity of each link."),
    P("Where are the piston velocity extremes in a slider-crank?", "The piston velocity is zero at the dead centres and reaches its maximum near the quarter-stroke position when the crank angle is roughly 90° from the dead centre.", "Zero at dead centres; maximum near mid-stroke.", "At TDC and BDC the crank and connecting rod are collinear, so the component of crank-pin velocity along the stroke is zero. At about 90° crank rotation the projection of the crank-pin velocity along the stroke is largest, giving the maximum piston velocity. This governs inertia forces and engine balance."),
    P("What is Coriolis acceleration?", "Coriolis acceleration a_c = 2·ω·v, where ω = angular velocity of the rotating guide and v = radial velocity of the slider relative to that guide. Its direction is found by rotating v through 90° in the direction of ω.", "a_c = 2ωv for a slider moving in a rotating guide.", "When a slider moves along a rotating link (e.g., Whitworth or shaper mechanisms), both the radius and the angle change, producing Coriolis acceleration a_c = 2ωv. In acceleration polygons it is drawn on the side toward which ω turns v. It occurs in any mechanism with a sliding joint on a rotating member."),
    P("State the laws of dry friction.", "(1) Friction opposes relative motion; (2) the limiting friction is proportional to the normal reaction, F = μN; (3) friction is independent of the apparent area of contact; (4) it depends on the materials in contact.", "Coulomb's laws: F = μN and independence from the contact area.", "Coulomb's laws describe dry sliding friction: at impending motion the friction force is μ times the normal reaction. It does not depend on the size of the contact patch but on the material pair and roughness. Kinetic friction uses μ_k, which is slightly smaller than μ_s. These laws break down for very smooth surfaces or high sliding speeds."),
    P("What is the angle of repose?", "The angle of repose is the maximum slope angle of an inclined plane on which a body just starts to slide down; at this angle tan θ = μ, so it equals the angle of friction.", "θ = arctan μ — the critical slope at which sliding begins.", "For a block on an incline at angle θ, sliding begins when mg sinθ = μ mg cosθ, i.e., tanθ = μ. Therefore the angle of repose equals the angle of friction φ = arctan μ. Granular materials form natural heaps at their angle of repose."),
    P("What is the angle of friction?", "The angle of friction φ is the angle made by the resultant reaction (normal plus friction) with the normal when motion is impending: tan φ = μ.", "φ = arctan μ — angle between the total reaction and the normal.", "When a body is about to slide, the surface reaction has normal component N and friction μN, so the resultant R makes angle φ with the normal, given by tanφ = F/N = μ. The cone of friction is the cone of constant angle φ; any force within the cone can be balanced."),
    P("A uniform ladder rests against a smooth wall. What is the friction requirement at the foot?", "For a uniform ladder of weight W leaning at angle θ against a smooth wall, the floor friction must reach μ ≥ 1/(2 tan θ) to prevent slipping.", "Moment equilibrium about the foot gives the wall reaction; floor friction balances it.", "Take moments about the foot: the wall reaction F_w acts horizontally at the top; W acts at mid-height. F_w·L sinθ = (WL/2)cosθ → F_w = W/(2 tanθ). The ground friction must balance F_w, so μ W ≥ W/(2 tanθ), hence μ ≥ 1/(2 tanθ). For θ = 60°, μ ≥ 0.289."),
    P("What is a wedge and how is the effort calculated?", "A wedge converts a small input force into a large transverse force. For a wedge of semi-angle α lifting a load W with friction angle φ at both surfaces, the effort P = W tan(α + 2φ).", "Wedge analysis carries friction at both contacting surfaces.", "Resolving at both the wedge-load and wedge-plane surfaces, each contact adds a friction angle φ, so the resulting relation for lifting is P = W tan(α + 2φ) (approximately). When α + 2φ > 90° the wedge is self-locking and stays in place even with the force removed, as in chucks and cotter joints."),
    P("What is the torque required to raise a load on a square-threaded screw?", "M = W·(d_m/2)·tan(α + φ), where d_m = mean screw diameter, tan α = l/(πd_m) is the helix angle, and tan φ = μ is the friction coefficient.", "Screw analysis treats the thread as an inclined plane wrapped round the shaft.", "A square-threaded screw is a helix, equivalent to an inclined plane of angle α at the mean radius. The effort at mean radius = W tan(α + φ); multiplying by the mean radius gives torque M = W(d_m/2)tan(α + φ). For lowering, M = W(d_m/2)tan(φ – α)."),
    P("What is the efficiency of a screw jack?", "η = tan α / tan(α + φ). Maximum efficiency is at α = 45° – φ/2, giving η_max = (1 – sin φ)/(1 + sin φ).", "Screw efficiency = tanα/tan(α+φ).", "The screw efficiency is the ratio of useful work to input: η = (W·l)/(2πM) = tanα/tan(α + φ). The best helix angle is α = 45° – φ/2 where η_max = (1 – sinφ)/(1 + sinφ). Square threads are more efficient than V-threads because there is no wedging of the flank forces."),
    P("When is a screw self-locking?", "A screw is self-locking when the friction angle exceeds the helix angle (φ > α), i.e., μ > tan α; then the load cannot unwind the screw on its own.", "Self-locking: φ > α, i.e., μ > tan α.", "If φ > α, then tan(φ – α) > 0, so a torque is needed even to lower the load; without it the load will not rotate the nut under gravity. Screw jacks are always self-locking. If φ < α the screw overhauls — the load runs down by itself, which is used in lathe lead screws to reduce backlash."),
    P("Compare square-thread and V-thread screws.", "For the same coefficient of friction, square threads have higher efficiency (no flank wedging), while V-threads grip better for clamping because the normal force is amplified by the half-apex angle, giving a higher effective friction.", "V-thread: effective friction μ/sin(β/2), higher grip, lower efficiency.", "In a V-thread of included angle 2β, the flank normal force is W/(sin β… larger than the axial load, so the friction is amplified: effective coefficient = μ/sin(β/2). This gives strong holding for fasteners but poorer efficiency for power screws. Square threads are preferred for motion (jacks, lead screws), V-threads for clamping."),
    P("What is pivot friction (flat pivot bearing)?", "For a flat pivot of radius r with axial load W, uniform pressure: T = (2/3)μWr; uniform wear: T = (½)μWr.", "Flat pivot: ⅔μWr (uniform pressure), ½μWr (uniform wear).", "For a flat-pivot thrust bearing, integrating sliding friction over annular rings gives the torque. The uniform-pressure model (T = ⅔μWr) suits a new bearing; the uniform-wear model (T = ½μWr, constant p·r) applies after running-in. Uniform wear gives lower torque and is used for lasting design."),
    P("What is collar friction (thrust collar) torque?", "For a collar bearing of radii r₁ and r₂: uniform pressure T = (2/3)μW(r₂³ – r₁³)/(r₂² – r₁²); uniform wear T = (½)μW(r₁ + r₂).", "Collar bearing: uniform wear gives T = ½μW(r1 + r2).", "A collar (thrust washer) supports axial load over an annulus from r₁ to r₂. Under uniform pressure the friction torque is T = (2/3)μW(r₂³ – r₁³)/(r₂² – r₁²). With uniform wear (constant pr), the torque simplifies to T = ½μW(r₁ + r₂), independent of the pressure distribution law apart from the radii."),
    P("Compare uniform pressure and uniform wear in thrust bearings.", "Uniform pressure (new bearing) gives higher torque concentrated near the outer radius; uniform wear (run-in bearing) gives lower torque, formula T = ½μW(r₁+r₂).", "Uniform wear predicts less torque and matches a worn bearing; design uses it.", "A new flat thrust bearing exerts uniform pressure; sliding wear removes material faster at the outer radius until p·r becomes constant (uniform wear). Uniform pressure gives T = ⅔μWr for a pivot, uniform wear ½μWr. In steady design, uniform-wear formulas are used because they describe the run-in condition."),
    P("What is the ratio of tensions in a belt drive?", "T₁/T₂ = e^(μθ), where T₁ and T₂ are the tight- and slack-side tensions, μ the friction coefficient, and θ the angle of lap in radians.", "T1/T2 = e^(μθ) — the belt-friction relation.", "Integrating the differential belt friction over the arc of contact gives ln(T₁/T₂) = μθ, hence T₁/T₂ = e^(μθ). This ratio limits how much tension difference the belt can transfer before slipping. Larger wrap angle θ or bigger μ increases the ratio and hence the power capacity."),
    P("What is centrifugal tension in a belt?", "Centrifugal tension T_c = m·v², where m = mass per unit length and v = belt speed. It acts equally on both sides of the belt.", "T_c = mv² — the belt must be tensioned to stay on the pulleys.", "A belt moving at speed v around a pulley is acted on by centripetal acceleration, producing a uniform tension T_c = mv² on both legs. The maximum usable tension is divided: T_max = T₁ + T_c. At high speeds centrifugal tension consumes a large share of the allowable stress, so the driving tension drops."),
    P("What is the initial tension in a belt drive?", "The initial (pre-run) tension T₀ ≈ (T₁ + T₂)/2. During power transmission the tight side tension becomes T₀ + ΔT and the slack side T₀ – ΔT.", "T₀ = (T1 + T2)/2 approximately.", "A belt is pre-tensioned to T₀ when stationary. In operation, transmitting torque raises one side and lowers the other so T₁ + T₂ = 2T₀, assuming the belt length does not change (CE of two legs). Centrifugal tension adds to the total: effective initial tension = T₀ + T_c."),
    P("What is the power transmitted by a belt drive?", "P = (T₁ – T₂)·v watts, where T₁ and T₂ are the tensions and v the belt speed. Equivalently, using pulley torque T = (T₁ – T₂)r, P = 2πNT/60.", "Power = effective tension difference × belt speed.", "The net tangential force on the pulley rim is (T₁ – T₂). Multiplying by the belt velocity gives power P = (T₁ – T₂)v. The centrifugal tension cancels from the difference (T₁–T_c)–(T₂–T_c), so power depends only on the difference of tight and slack tensions and the speed."),
    P("What is the condition for maximum power transmission by a belt?", "Maximum power occurs when the centrifugal tension equals one third of the maximum tension: T_c = T_max/3, corresponding to v = √(T_max/(3m)).", "Max power: T_c = Tmax/3, so v = √(Tmax/3m).", "With T₁ = T_max – T_c and T₂ = T₁/e^(μθ), power P = T₁(1 – e^(–μθ))v. Substituting T_c = mv² and differentiating with respect to v gives T_c = T_max/3 at maximum power, hence v_opt = √(T_max/(3m)). This optimum is used to choose belt speed for best power."),
    P("What is slip in a belt drive?", "Slip is relative motion between the belt and the pulley surface; it reduces the velocity ratio. With slip s₁ and s₂ on the two pulleys, N₂/N₁ = (d₁/d₂)(1 – s₁ – s₂).", "Slip lowers the speed ratio below the geometric value.", "Owing to the elastic extension difference, part of the arc is a driving (elastic) arc and beyond it the belt slips if tension is too high. The velocity ratio with slip: N₂/N₁ = (d₁/d₂)(1 – s₁ – s₂). Slip is different from creep, which is the continuous stretching of the belt over the arc of contact."),
    P("What is creep in a belt drive?", "Creep is the differential stretching of the belt as it passes from the tight to the slack side over the arc of contact; it always occurs in elastic belts and slightly reduces the velocity ratio.", "Creep = belt stretches/contracts over the wrap; unavoidable in elastic belts.", "As the belt runs over a pulley, its tension decreases from T₁ to T₂, so it shortens. This length change causes relative motion (creep) over an arc of contact. Creep cannot be eliminated and is one reason flat belts have speed ratios slightly below ideal. Slip is gross sliding and can be prevented by correct tension."),
    P("Compare V-belt and flat belt drives.", "A V-belt grips by wedging in the groove, giving ratio T₁/T₂ = e^(μθ/sin(α/2)), so it transmits more power at the same tension. Flat belts need larger wrap or higher tension.", "V-belt wedging multiplies friction by 1/sin(α/2).", "For a V-belt in a groove with included angle 2α, the effective mid-groove normal is amplified by the wedge factor 1/sin(α/2), so the tension ratio becomes e^(μθ/sin(α/2)). For α/2 = 20°, this factor is about 2.92, roughly tripling friction capacity. V-belts are compact and alignment-tolerant; flat belts suit very high-speed light-load drives."),
    P("What is the speed ratio of a belt drive?", "N₂/N₁ = d₁/d₂ (neglecting slip), since both pulleys run at the same belt speed v = πdN/60.", "Velocity ratio = inverse of diameter ratio.", "The belt runs without relative motion in the driving arc, so πd₁N₁/60 = πd₂N₂/60, giving N₂ = N₁(d₁/d₂). With slip the ratio is reduced. For flat belts the rule of thumb is a speed ratio up to about 3–6; beyond that, multi-drive or gear stages are economical."),
    P("What is the effect of belt speed on power capacity?", "For fixed tensions, power = (T₁ – T₂)v rises with speed, but centrifugal tension grows as v² and drains the tight side, so there is an optimum speed v = √(T_max/3m) for maximum power.", "Power grows with v then falls because of the v² centrifugal term.", "Up to v_opt = √(T_max/3m), increasing the belt speed raises the power nearly linearly; beyond it, the centrifugal tension term mv² consumes too much of the allowable stress and power falls. Hence high-speed belts are made light and thin to keep m low."),
    P("What is a chain drive and its advantages over a belt drive?", "A chain drive transmits power through positive engagement of chain links with sprocket teeth — no slip, constant velocity ratio, high torque capacity, higher efficiency (about 97–98%), and compactness.", "Positive engagement gives no slip and an exact ratio.", "Chains engage the sprocket teeth positively, so there is neither slip nor creep and the velocity ratio is fixed. They carry heavy torque at moderate speed and are efficient and compact. Their drawbacks are the need for lubrication, inevitable elongation, and noise. Used in bicycles, conveyors, and engine timing drives."),
    P("What is chain pitch and why does it matter?", "Chain pitch is the centre-to-centre distance between adjacent pins in a chain. Small pitch gives smoother, quieter running; large pitch carries more power but is noisier and wears faster.", "Pitch = distance between successive pin centres (the chain module).", "The pitch p of a chain matches the sprocket chord between teeth; the pitch circle radius r satisfies p = 2r sin(π/z) for z teeth. A short-pitch chain (many small sprockets) runs smoother at high speed; long pitch chains are strong but rough. Chain pitch is standardized by the industry in fractions of an inch (e.g., ½ inch roller chain)."),
    P("Why prefer chains to gears for long centres?", "Chains transmit power positively over comparatively long centre distances (several metres) without the alignment cost of large gears or the slip of belts.", "Chains bridge long distances flexibly; gears need rigid close centres.", "Gears mesh rigidly at closely controlled centres; putting them far apart needs costly accurate frames. A chain loops flexibly between sprockets over long spans, tolerates slight misalignment, and still gives positive (slip-free) transmission. For very long distances, idler sprockets are added. Thus chains fit conveyors, bicycles, and camshaft drives."),
    P("What is a simple gear train?", "A simple gear train has one gear per shaft; the velocity ratio is just the ratio of the teeth of the last and first gears, and intermediate gears only change the direction.", "Simple train: one gear per shaft; ratio = Z_last/Z_first.", "In a simple gear train every intermediate gear is on its own shaft. The ratio ω_first/ω_last = Z_last/Z_first (product of teeth ratios cancels along the chain). An idler gear reverses direction but does not change the ratio. External gear pairs reverse direction; internal (annular) pairs do not."),
    P("What is a compound gear train?", "A compound gear train has two gears fixed on common intermediate shafts; the velocity ratio equals the product of the driven teeth divided by the product of the driver teeth.", "Compound train: paired gears on shared shafts give large ratios.", "In a compound train, gears are keyed in pairs so each shaft carries two gears. The overall ratio is (Z₂/Z₁)×(Z₄/Z₃)×..., which can be made very large in a compact space. Example: 20 drives 60 (2:1), then 25 drives 100 (4:1) → total 8:1 reduction."),
    P("What is a reverted gear train?", "A reverted gear train has the input and output shafts coaxial; the centre distances of the successive gear pairs are equal, so (Z₁ + Z₂)/2 = (Z₃ + Z₄)/2 for equal modules.", "Reverted train: output shaft is on the same axis as the input.", "In a reverted train the final gear is on the same axis as the first, so the sum of the radii of each meshing pair must be equal: for module m, m(Z₁+Z₂)/2 = m(Z₃+Z₄)/2. Such trains are used in back gears of lathes, clock mechanisms, and simple gear boxes requiring coaxial input and output."),
    P("What is an epicyclic (planetary) gear train?", "An epicyclic gear train has one or more gear axes carried by a rotating arm (carrier); gears revolve about the central sun gear and mesh with an annulus. It gives high reduction and differential action.", "Epicyclic: gear axes rotate with the arm — like planets round the sun.", "In an epicyclic train the planet gears are carried by a rotating arm, so their axes revolve. The sun gear, planet gears, and internal ring produce large or differential speed ratios with coaxial input/output. They are found in automatic gearboxes and differentials. Speeds are analysed by the tabular or Willis method."),
    P("What is the tabular method for epicyclic gear trains?", "The tabular method solves the train by giving the arm a step, finding the relative rotations and compounding, then adding the arm rotation to each gear to get absolute speeds.", "Build the relative-motion table, then add the arm speed back.", "Tabulate: first give a unit rotation to one gear with the arm locked, list relative speeds of all gears, then add the arm speed to all. The absolute speed of each gear = arm speed + train-value × relative speed. The tabular method is simple and reliable for any epicyclic arrangement including compound planets."),
    P("What is the train value of a gear train?", "The train value is the speed ratio of the last gear to the first gear: for simple trains, = product of driven teeth ÷ product of driver teeth (with sign for direction).", "Train value = N_out/N_in; the driving-to-driven teeth product ratio.", "For a simple or compound gear train, train value = (Z_driven product)/(Z_driver product) with a negative sign for each external gear pair (reversal) and positive for internal pairs. For epicyclic trains, the train value is expressed with the arm held fixed. The ratio governs speed, torque, and direction of the output."),
    P("State the law of gearing.", "For constant velocity ratio transmission, the common normal to the tooth profiles at the point of contact must pass through the pitch point.", "The common normal at the contact must pass through the pitch point.", "According to the law of gearing, if the normal at the contact point of meshing teeth passes through the pitch point, the velocity ratio is constant: ω₁/ω₂ = O₂P/O₁P, where O₁, O₂ are the gear centres and P is the pitch point. Involute and cycloidal profiles are designed to satisfy this law at every contact position."),
    P("Describe the involute tooth profile.", "An involute is traced by a point on a taut string unwound from a base circle; its common normal is tangent to both base circles, giving constant velocity ratio even if the centre distance varies.", "Involute: the line of action is tangent to both base circles.", "The involute of a circle is the locus of a point on a taut cord unwound from the base circle. The common normal at contact (the line of action) is tangent to both base circles and passes through the pitch point, satisfying the law of gearing. Involute gears keep a constant velocity ratio even with small changes in centre distance — their great practical advantage."),
    P("Describe the cycloidal tooth profile.", "Cycloidal teeth use an epicycloid for the face (above the pitch circle) and a hypocycloid for the flank; both are generated by the same rolling circle. They are quieter and stronger but need exact centre distance.", "Cycloidal: epicycloid face and hypocycloid flank from one rolling circle.", "In cycloidal gearing, the path of contact is two intersecting circular arcs; the tooth face is an epicycloid and the flank a hypocycloid traced by a common generating (rolling) circle. Cycloidal teeth have less interference and run quietly, but they require the exact centre distance, so modern gearing almost always uses involute teeth which tolerate centre-distance errors."),
    P("What is the module of a gear?", "Module m = pitch circle diameter / number of teeth = d/N (in mm). The circular pitch p_c = πm. Meshing gears must have equal modules.", "Module m = d/N — the metric tooth-size unit.", "The module is the metric size of the gear teeth: m = d/N. The circular pitch, the arc distance between corresponding points of adjacent teeth on the pitch circle, is p_c = πd/N = πm. Standard modules are 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10 mm, etc. Two gears mesh only when their modules (and pressure angles) match."),
    P("What are circular pitch, diametral pitch, and module?", "Circular pitch p_c = πd/N mm. Diametral pitch P_d = N/d teeth per inch. Relation p_c×P_d = π. Module m = d/N = 25.4/P_d mm.", "p·P = π; m = 25.4/P_d.", "The circular pitch is the arc length per tooth on the pitch circle. The diametral pitch (US units) is the number of teeth per inch of pitch diameter, p_c = π/P_d. The module (metric) = pitch diameter in mm ÷ teeth = 25.4/P_d. Larger module → larger teeth and higher bending strength."),
    P("What is the pressure angle of a gear?", "The pressure angle (standard 20°, old 14.5°) is the angle between the line of action and the common tangent to the pitch circles at the pitch point.", "Pressure angle: slope of the line of action relative to the pitch tangent.", "The line of action is the common normal at contact (tangent to the base circles). Its angle with the common tangent to the pitch circles is the pressure angle. At 20° teeth are stronger and the interference tendency is less than at 14.5°. Bearings see a radial component of the contact force proportional to tan φ."),
    P("What is gear interference and the minimum number of teeth to avoid it?", "Interference is the digging of one tooth tip into the non-involute flank of the other; for straight involute gears it is avoided if z_min ≥ 2/sin²φ (rack and pinion limit). For φ = 20°, z_min ≈ 17; for 14.5°, z_min ≈ 32.", "Minimum teeth for 20° full-depth involute ≈ 17; for 14.5° ≈ 32.", "Interference occurs when the line of action extends beyond the base-circle tangency point and the trochoidal part of the flank collides with the tip. The minimum number of pinion teeth for a rack is z_min = 2/sin²φ: at φ = 20° this is about 17 and at 14.5° about 32. Undercutting occurs when the pinion has fewer teeth than this limit."),
    P("What is backlash in gears?", "Backlash is the clearance between the non-working faces of meshing teeth, giving a small lost motion, or free play, when the direction of rotation reverses.", "Backlash = clearance on the back side = play on reversal.", "Backlash is deliberately provided because the tooth space must be slightly larger than the tooth thickness to allow lubrication, thermal growth, and manufacturing tolerances. It is harmless in unidirectional drives but introduces lost motion on reversal. Precision instruments use spring-loaded anti-backlash gears."),
    P("What are addendum, dedendum, and whole depth of gear teeth?", "Addendum a = 1×m (standard full depth), dedendum b = 1.25×m, working depth = 2m, whole depth = 2.25m, clearance = 0.25m.", "Addendum 1m, dedendum 1.25m, whole depth 2.25m.", "The tooth top above the pitch circle is the addendum (1 module), and the depth below the pitch circle to the root is the dedendum (1.25 module). The working depth of engagement is 2m and the whole depth 2.25m, with 0.25m tip clearance. Stub teeth use shorter addenda (0.8m) to reduce interference."),
    P("What is the velocity ratio of two meshing gears?", "ω₁/ω₂ = Z₂/Z₁ = d₂/d₁, where Z = teeth numbers and d = pitch diameters.", "Velocity ratio = inverse of the teeth ratio.", "At the pitch point, the tangential pitch-line velocities of both gears are equal: ω₁d₁/2 = ω₂d₂/2, giving ω₁/ω₂ = d₂/d₁ = Z₂/Z₁. Since tooth numbers are integers, gears give exact constant ratios. A small pinion driving a large gear reduces speed and multiplies torque by the same factor."),
    P("What is the torque transmitted by a single-plate clutch?", "For uniform wear: T = ½μW(r₁ + r₂), where μ = friction coefficient, W = axial spring load, r₁, r₂ = inner and outer radii. For uniform pressure: T = ⅔μW(r₂³ – r₁³)/(r₂² – r₁²).", "Single plate, uniform wear: T = ½μW(r1 + r2).", "A single-plate dry clutch (car) transmits torque by friction on annular surfaces pressed together with force W. Integrating μ·p·r·dA over the annulus, uniform wear gives T = ½μW(r₁+r₂) whereas uniform pressure gives T = ⅔μW(r₂³–r₁³)/(r₂²–r₁²). The clutch is sized so this torque exceeds the maximum engine torque."),
    P("What is a multi-plate clutch and its torque formula?", "A multi-plate clutch stacks several friction pairs; torque T = n·½μW(r₁+r₂) for uniform wear, where n = number of friction pairs (contact faces). It transmits high torque in a compact package.", "Multi-plate: torque multiplied by the number of friction pairs.", "Stacking n friction surfaces multiplies the torque capacity n-fold for a given diameter and axial load: T = n·½μW(r₁+r₂). Multi-plate clutches suit motorcycles, heavy vehicles, and racing gearboxes where space is limited. Their drawbacks are cooling and the quantity of plates."),
    P("What is the torque capacity of a cone clutch?", "Cone clutch torque (uniform pressure) T = μW r / sin α, where α is the semi-cone angle; for uniform wear T = ½μW(r₁+r₂)/sin α.", "Cone clutch: T = μWr/sinα; a steeper cone (smaller α) grips harder.", "The conical friction surface turns part of the axial thrust into normal force W/sin α, so the torque is T = μ·(W/sinα)·r for uniform pressure. Smaller semi-angle α increases capacity but small α can make the clutch self-energising or self-engaging; practical design uses α ≈ 12–15°."),
    P("What is a centrifugal clutch?", "A centrifugal clutch engages automatically when the shaft speed makes the friction shoes fly outward against the drum; below the engagement speed the shoes are pulled back by springs and the drive is free.", "Shoes are thrown outward by centrifugal force at preset speed to grip the drum.", "In a centrifugal clutch, shoes on the driver press the inner drum face when the centrifugal force mω²r overcomes the return springs. The contact (normal) force = mω²r – spring force; torque = μN·r_drum. Such clutches give smooth automatic engagement and are used on lawn mowers, scooters, and engine couplings."),
    P("What is a block (shoe) brake?", "A block brake presses a friction block against a rotating drum; the braking torque is μN·r, and the lever geometry determines whether the friction force assists (self-energising) or opposes the actuation.", "Block brake: friction block pressed on the drum; check self-locking.", "A block brake has a shoe pivoted on a lever; pressing the block against the drum produces a friction force μN. Braking torque = μN·r_drum. The friction force creates a moment about the lever pivot: if it helps the applied force the brake is self-energising; if it can hold the drum alone, the brake is self-locking."),
    P("What is a band brake and its torque formula?", "A band brake wraps a flexible band round the drum; T₁/T₂ = e^(μθ), and braking torque T_b = (T₁ – T₂)r_drum, also P_b = torque × ω.", "Band brake: T1/T2 = e^(μθ), brake torque = (T1–T2)r.", "The band tensions differ by the friction ratio e^(μθ); the net force (T₁ – T₂) at the drum radius gives the braking torque (T₁ – T₂)·r. The lever is usually connected to the tight side so the friction force does not add to the applied force (avoiding self-locking). Band brakes are simple, powerful, and suit hoists and cranes."),
    P("What is a band and block brake?", "A band and block brake has wooden blocks riveted to the band; each block raises the tension by the factor (1 + μ tanφ)/(1 – μ tanφ). For n blocks: T₁/T₂ = ((1+μ tanφ)/(1–μ tanφ))ⁿ, where 2φ is the block face angle.", "Band and block: more blocks → larger tension ratio for the same wrap.", "The block contacts the drum over a face making angle 2φ at the centre; the friction amplification per block is (1 + μ tanφ)/(1 – μ tanφ). Multiplying over n blocks: T₁/T₂ = thatⁿ. This increases the torque capacity of a given wrap compared with a plain band. Braking torque = (T₁ – T₂)·r."),
    P("What is an internal-expanding shoe brake?", "An internal-expanding shoe brake uses two shoes inside the drum which are expanded outward by hydraulic pistons or a cam against the drum; the braking torque is the sum of the shoe moments. Used in vehicle wheels.", "Shoes expand outward inside the wheel drum — vehicle brake.", "In an internal-expanding brake, the shoes inside the rotating drum are forced out by hydraulic cylinders or a cam. Each shoe is analysed like a block brake in which the friction moment helps (leading shoe, self-energising) or opposes (trailing shoe). Total torque = Σ μN_i·r over the two shoes. The leading shoe does most of the work."),
    P("What are self-locking and self-energising brakes?", "A self-energising brake is one in which the friction force assists the operating force (less effort needed). A self-locking brake holds the drum by friction alone with zero actuating force.", "Friction assists actuation (self-energising); if it alone holds, the brake is self-locking.", "If the friction force moment about the shoe pivot adds to the applied moment, the brake is self-energising: the required handle force falls as friction grows. When that friction moment alone can hold the drum, the brake is self-locking — dangerous on vehicles because released brakes would re-grip. Self-locking brakes suit hoists and cranes where unplanned release must never happen."),
    P("What is a differential band brake?", "In a differential band brake the ends of the band are attached to the lever at different radii; the acting moment is the difference of the two band-end moments, giving high leverage in a small lever.", "Both band ends act on the lever; their moments subtract.", "In a differential band brake, the tight end T₁ and slack end T₂ are fixed to the lever on opposite sides (or at different radii). The net lever moment = T₁·a – T₂·b, so a small handle force can balance a large drum torque. Care is needed: if the geometry reverses, the brake can become self-locking."),
    P("What is a dynamometer and what are its types?", "A dynamometer measures the power of an engine or machine. Absorption dynamometers absorb the whole power (Prony brake, rope brake, Froude hydraulic dynamometer); transmission dynamometers measure torque in a drive without absorbing it (belt or torsion dynamometer).", "Absorption: dissipates the power; transmission: measures it in line.", "Absorption dynamometers load the engine with friction or hydraulic resistance and measure the resisting torque; power = 2πNT/60. Transmission dynamometers read the torque flowing through the driveline (e.g., by the twist of a torsion shaft or belt forces) without consuming the power. Absorption types are simpler and used for testing; transmission types monitor in service."),
    P("How does a Prony brake dynamometer work?", "The Prony brake absorbs power by friction: a brake block resists the drum and the tangential force F measured on a lever arm of length r gives torque T = F·r; power P = 2πNT/60.", "Prony brake: T = F·r from the lever-arm force; P = 2πNT/60.", "The drum is braked by a block or band; the resisting force F is measured at a known lever radius r. Torque T = F·r and power P = 2πNT/60. It is an absorption dynamometer — the power is converted to heat at the brake. Simple, reliable, and standard for moderate-speed engine testing."),
    P("What is a torsion (transmission) dynamometer?", "A torsion dynamometer measures the twist angle θ of a shaft of known torsional rigidity GJ: T = GJ·θ/L, and P = 2πNT/60, without absorbing the power.", "Measure the shaft twist; T = GJθ/L.", "The shaft twists under torque according to θ = TL/(GJ). A torsion meter measures θ over length L, giving T = GJθ/L, then power from P = 2πNT/60. Because it only measures the twist, it does not absorb power, so it is a transmission dynamometer used on running machinery."),
    P("What is a cam and a follower?", "A cam is a rotating or reciprocating machine element with a curved profile that imparts the required motion to a follower that rides on it by direct contact.", "Cam = driver with curved profile; follower = element it drives.", "Cams convert rotary (or oscillatory/translating) motion into a specified follower motion schedule of rise, dwell, and return. The contact is through a higher pair (point or line). Cams control the valves of IC engines, fuel pumps, machine tools, and automatic machines, where timing and profile precision are essential."),
    P("What are the types of cam followers?", "Follower ends: knife-edge, roller, flat-face (mushroom), and spherical-face. Cams: rotating disc cams, translating cams, cylindrical cams. The roller follower is the most common; flat-face avoids undercutting at high speed.", "Knife, roller, flat and spherical ends; roller is most used.", "The knife-edge follower is accurate for profiling but wears fast. The roller follower replaces sliding by rolling, reducing friction and wear, and is most common, but its radius must be less than the minimum cam radius of curvature. Flat-face followers accept large loads at low contact stress and suit high-speed cams where undercutting would occur with roller."),
    P("What is the base circle of a cam?", "The base circle is the smallest circle centred on the cam rotation that is tangent to the cam profile; the follower rests on it during the dwell at the lowest position. A larger base circle reduces pressure angle and flank loads.", "Base circle sets the smallest cam radius; big circle reduces pressure angle.", "The base circle is the datum radius of the cam; during dwell the follower rests on it. A large base circle lowers the pressure angle and the contact stresses, reducing flank (side) thrust on the follower guide, at the cost of a bigger cam. The pressure angle stays near the base-circle end during rise and return; the profile is generated offset from the base circle by the lift calculations."),
    P("Describe the uniform velocity cam displacement diagram.", "In a uniform (constant) velocity cam, the follower displacement is linear with cam rotation, so its velocity is constant; at the ends of the strokes the velocity changes abruptly, implying infinite acceleration and impact shocks. It is used only at low speed.", "Constant velocity: straight-line displacement; infinite jerk at stroke ends.", "For uniform-velocity motion, y = (h/θ_rise)·θ so the follower velocity is constant during rise and constant (different) during return. The discontinuities at the start, end, and reversal of the stroke give theoretically infinite acceleration — a shock (jerk). Such cams are limited to low-speed duty; higher speeds use SHM or cycloidal profiles."),
    P("Describe the simple harmonic motion (SHM) cam profile.", "For SHM cam motion the follower displacement follows a sinusoid: y = (h/2)(1 – cos(πθ/β)) during the rise over angle β. Acceleration is zero at the ends but peaks at mid-stroke.", "SHM: y = (h/2)(1 – cosθ); smooth start and finish, peak acceleration mid-stroke.", "In SHM motion, the displacement rises sinusoidally so the follower starts and stops with zero acceleration (no jerk) but reaches a maximum acceleration in the middle of the stroke. The acceleration magnitude is higher than for cycloidal, so SHM cams suit moderate speeds. y = (h/2)(1 – cos(πθ/β)) is the rise law."),
    P("Describe the cycloidal cam motion.", "Cycloidal motion gives acceleration following a sine curve that is zero at both ends of the stroke, so both velocity and acceleration start and finish at zero; displacement y = h[(θ/β) – (sin(2πθ/β))/(2π)]. It is the smoothest and is used for high-speed cams.", "Cycloidal: sinusoidal acceleration with zero at both ends — smoothest cam motion.", "In cycloidal motion the displacement curve is a cycloid projected on the stroke: y = h[(θ/β) – (sin(2πθ/β))/(2π)]. Its acceleration is a sine wave with zero values at beginning and end, giving zero jerk at the extremes. Among the standard motions, cycloidal causes the smallest dynamic force peaks, so it is preferred above about 1000 cam rpm."),
    P("What is dwell and the angle of dwell of a cam?", "Dwell is the interval during which the follower remains stationary at a specified position while the cam continues to rotate. The angle of dwell is the cam angle for which the follower is at rest (at the base circle or at full lift).", "Dwell = follower stationary for a definite cam angle.", "A cam cycle comprises rise, dwell, return, and dwell. The dwell duration is set by the follower timing requirements (e.g., the period a fuel-injection plunger must stay at its lifted position). Longer dwell with the same cycle often needs a larger cam or special profiles. Dwell exists both at the low (base circle) and the high (lifted) positions."),
    P("Why does a high-speed cam suffer follower jump?", "At high speed the follower inertia force m·a exceeds the spring force holding it on the cam, so the follower leaves the cam surface (jump), causing noise, impact, and loss of timing. The jump speed depends on the peak acceleration.", "High acceleration → inertia exceeds spring force → follower leaves the cam.", "The cam keeps the follower in contact only while the controlling (spring) force exceeds the required inertia force m·a. When the peak acceleration is too large, contact is lost and the follower jumps; it lands with impact. Remedies: use cycloidal motion (lowest acceleration peaks), larger base circle, stronger springs, or positive-drive (grooved) cams. Jump is a classic high-speed-cam design question."),
    P("What is a governor and what is its purpose?", "A governor is an automatic device that senses speed changes and regulates the fuel/steam supply so the engine speed stays roughly constant under varying loads.", "Governor senses speed change and adjusts the fuel input.", "Governors measure speed through centrifugal weights (or springs) and move a sleeve that controls a throttle or fuel rack. When load drops, speed rises, the sleeve moves and reduces fuel; when load rises, speed falls and fuel is increased. Governors respond over a range of speeds and loads, unlike a flywheel, which merely smooths cyclic speed fluctuation."),
    P("What is the height of a Watt governor?", "For a Watt governor, h = g/ω² = 895/N² metres, where N is the speed in rpm (neglecting friction and link weights).", "Watt governor: h = g/ω² ≈ 895/N² m.", "The Watt (first-order) governor is a simple conical pendulum pair. At a sleeve height h, the centrifugal force balances the ball weight giving tan(θ/2)… the height h = g/ω² independent of the ball mass or arm length. Converting to rpm: h ≈ 895/N² m. At 60 rpm, h ≈ 0.25 m; at 100 rpm, h ≈ 0.09 m."),
    P("What is a Porter governor and its height formula?", "The Porter governor adds a central dead weight (sleeve mass M); for equal arms the height h = (895/N²)(1 + M/(2m)), where m = ball mass.", "Porter: h = (895/N²)(1 + M/(2m)).", "The Porter governor carries the loaded sleeve of mass M on the central spindle; the dead weight increases the controlling force at the sleeve. Taking moments about the pivot, h = (g/ω²)(1 + M/(2m)). Substituting N in rpm: h = (895/N²)(1 + M/(2m)). Adding the dead weight makes the governor run at lower height for the same speed and increases its sensitivity."),
    P("What is a Proell governor?", "The Proell governor is a Porter governor modified by mounting the balls on extensions of the links beyond the pivots (inclined arms), giving a larger radius of rotation for a given height and an improved range of speed.", "Proell: ball radius extended beyond the link pivots — more leverage.", "In the Proell governor the ball is attached to extensions of the lower arms, so the effective radius at the ball centre is larger than a plain Porter with the same height. The larger radius magnifies the centrifugal force moment, giving higher sensitivity within a compact construction. The equilibrium analysis uses moments of ball and dead-weight forces about the bell-crank pivot."),
    P("What is a Hartnell governor?", "The Hartnell governor is a spring-controlled governor with two bell-crank levers whose balls are loaded by a helical spring; the spring force replaces the dead weight, giving a compact unit suited to high speeds.", "Hartnell: spring-loaded bell crank governor, adjustable speed range.", "In the Hartnell governor the sleeve is moved by the spring preload and rate against the centrifugal force of the balls on the bell-crank levers. Governing is set by the spring stiffness and the initial compression. Its advantages: compact, operates at high speed, and the speed range is adjusted by changing spring load. The equilibrium equation is F_c = mω²r, equated to spring force components."),
    P("What is isochronism of a governor?", "A governor is isochronous when its equilibrium speed is the same for all sleeve positions, meaning it maintains a constant speed at every load — an ideal condition not exactly achievable in practice.", "Isochronous governor: the same speed at all sleeve positions.", "If the equilibrium speed of a governor is identical over the whole sleeve travel, it is isochronous: the engine would run at exactly one speed for any load setting. A spring-controlled governor can be made approximately isochronous over a small sleeve range. Isochronism is a boundary of stability — beyond it the governor becomes unstable and hunts."),
    P("Define sensitivity, stability, and hunting of a governor.", "Sensitivity = (Nmax – Nmin)/Nmean. A stable governor gives a definite sleeve position for each speed. Hunting is persistent, growing oscillation of the sleeve about its mean position due to excessive sensitivity and fuel lag.", "Sensitivity = ΔN/Nmean; hunting = continuous amplified sleeve oscillation.", "Sensitivity measures responsivity: how much the sleeve moves for a given speed change. A stable governor always settles to the same sleeve position for the same speed. If the governor is too sensitive, small speed changes produce large fuel corrections that overshoot and oscillate with growing amplitude — this is hunting, damaging to engine speed control. Design seeks high sensitivity without hunting."),
    P("What is the effort of a governor?", "The effort of a governor is the average force exerted at the sleeve for a one per cent change of speed from the mean speed.", "Effort = sleeve force developed per 1% speed change.", "The effort E is the mean sleeve force associated with a 1% change in mean speed; it is obtained by differentiating the sleeve-force/speed relation, δF = (change in sleeve force)/(0.01×sleeve lift). The effort must overcome the throttle-linkage resistance. For a Watt governor E = (9.81η…) formulas appear in standard texts."),
    P("What is the power of a governor?", "The power of a governor is the work done at the sleeve for a 1% speed change: Power = Effort × Sleeve displacement corresponding to the 1% speed change.", "Power = effort × sleeve lift for 1% speed change.", "The power of a governor measures its ability to move the control gear. It equals the effort multiplied by the sleeve stroke produced by raising the speed 1% above the mean. This value ensures the governor can shift the throttle/fuel rack against its spring and friction. It grows with the sleeve travel and the dead weight or spring force."),
    P("Compare dead-weight (centrifugal) and spring-controlled governors.", "Dead-weight governors (Watt, Porter, Proell) use gravity for the controlling force and are heavier; spring-controlled governors (Hartnell, Hartung) are compact, respond faster, and can operate at high speeds, but their characteristics depend on spring rate and preload.", "Spring governors are compact and fast; gravity governors are bulky and slower.", "The controlling force in centrifugal governors comes from gravity on the balls and sleeve; dead-weight types are heavy and slow-moving. Spring-controlled governors store the controlling force in a spring, so they are lighter, work at high speeds, and allow tuning of the speed range by spring preload. Fuel-injection pumps and high-speed engines therefore use spring governors."),
    P("What is a flywheel and how does it differ from a governor?", "A flywheel stores and releases kinetic energy within each cycle to limit the cyclic speed fluctuation; a governor adjusts the energy input to control the mean speed over many cycles.", "Flywheel smooths within-cycle fluctuation; governor controls mean speed.", "The flywheel (large inertia) absorbs energy when torque output exceeds demand and restores it when deficient, reducing speed variation within each revolution. It cannot act over long load changes. The governor senses long-term speed deviation and adjusts the fuel/steam supply. Flywheels and governors therefore work together: flywheel for cyclic, governor for average-speed control."),
    P("What is the fluctuation of energy of a flywheel?", "The fluctuation of energy ΔE is the greatest difference between the maximum and minimum energy stored in the flywheel during a cycle, determined from the turning moment diagram: ΔE = I·ω²·C_s.", "ΔE = Iω²·C_s, read from the extremes of the turning moment diagram.", "From the turning moment diagram the cumulative energy at each crank angle is plotted; the difference between its maximum and minimum is the fluctuation of energy ΔE. Relating it to the speed swing: ΔE = Iω²(ω_max–ω_min)/ω = Iω²C_s. The flywheel mass is chosen to give the required I for a permitted C_s: I = ΔE/(ω²C_s)."),
    P("What is the coefficient of fluctuation of speed?", "C_s = (ω_max – ω_min)/ω_mean = 2(ω_max–ω_min)/(ω_max + ω_mean×2…) i.e., 2(ω₁–ω₂)/(ω₁+ω₂). Smaller C_s needs a heavier flywheel.", "C_s = (Nmax – Nmin)/Nmean — the permitted speed swing ratio.", "The coefficient of fluctuation of speed is the ratio of the maximum speed swing to the mean speed: C_s = (ω_max – ω_min)/ω_mean. For generators C_s ≈ 0.002, for pumps 0.03–0.05. Since I = ΔE/(ω_mean²·C_s), a smaller C_s demands more flywheel inertia and mass. Flywheels are sized by the allowable speed fluctuation."),
    P("What is the coefficient of fluctuation of energy?", "C_E = ΔE/E_mean, the ratio of the maximum fluctuation of energy to the mean energy (work) per cycle.", "C_E = ΔE/E_mean — a cycle-energy property from the TMD.", "The coefficient of fluctuation of energy compares the energy swing ΔE (read from the turning moment diagram) with the mean energy delivered per cycle E_mean = mean torque × 2π. Typical values: steam engine C_E ≈ 0.07–0.1; single-cylinder I.C. engine higher; multi-cylinder and turbine engines much smaller (0.002–0.01)."),
    P("What is a turning moment diagram?", "A turning moment diagram plots crankshaft torque (turning moment) against crank angle over one cycle; the area under it equals the work per cycle, and the area above/below the mean torque gives the energy fluctuation.", "TMD: torque vs crank angle; area = work; peaks give ΔE.", "The turning moment diagram shows how the turning moment on the crank varies with crank position. A horizontal line of mean torque divides surplus and deficit areas. Accumulating these areas gives the energy curve; its extremes span the fluctuation of energy ΔE. For multi-cylinder engines the diagram is flatter, requiring a smaller flywheel."),
    P("Why does a punching press need a large flywheel?", "A punching press does its shearing work in a very short part of the press cycle, producing a huge instantaneous torque; a flywheel offsets the motor torque, so a small motor drives the job while the flywheel supplies the peak energy.", "Short, high peak load → flywheel stores energy between strokes.", "In a punching press the metal shears within a small crank-angle range, demanding a torque spike many times the average motor torque. The flywheel stores energy during the idle part of the stroke and releases it during piercing. This allows a small motor rating; the energy needed for the hole, minus motor work during the punch, is drawn from the flywheel's stored energy."),
    P("What is the flywheel rim design rule?", "The flywheel rim stores nearly all the energy; using hoop stress σ = ρv² (mean radius theory), the rim cross-section is sized so the peripheral velocity is limited, and with mass m = I/k² the rim carries the required inertia.", "Rim energy = ½m v²; hoop stress limits the rim speed.", "For a flywheel rim, all mass is concentrated at radius R, so I = mR² and the stored energy = ½Iω² = ½mv². The allowable rim speed is set by the hoop stress σ = ρv² (density ρ). Hence the rim coefficient (peripheral speed) is limited and the cross-section area is chosen to give the required mass and safe speed."),
    P("What is static balancing of rotating masses?", "Static balancing ensures the resultant of all centrifugal forces of the masses acting on the shaft is zero: vector sum of (m·r) terms = 0. The centre of mass lies on the shaft axis.", "Static balance: Σm·r = 0 in the single reference plane.", "For masses rotating with a shaft, each produces centrifugal force mω²r. Static balancing sets Σm·r = 0 so the shaft is at equilibrium when at rest. It is done in one plane with a trial mass. However, static balance alone does not prevent dynamic (couple) imbalance when masses lie in different planes."),
    P("What is dynamic balancing?", "Dynamic balancing requires both the resultant force and the resultant couple to vanish: vector Σm·r = 0 and Σm·r·l = 0 (couple about any point). Balancing masses are needed in two planes.", "Dynamic: both force and couple polygons must close (two planes).", "If centrifugal forces of masses in different planes are statically balanced but not collinear, they form a couple which whirls the shaft at speed. Dynamic balancing adds masses in two separate planes so the force polygon and the couple polygon both close. Rotors that are long relative to their diameter require dynamic balancing."),
    P("What is the primary force in a reciprocating engine (slider-crank)?", "The primary shaking force is F_p = m·ω²·r·cosθ acting along the line of stroke, where m = reciprocating mass, ω = crank speed, r = crank radius, θ = crank angle.", "Primary force = mω²r cosθ — the main reciprocating inertia force.", "Approximating the slider position, the primary acceleration of the reciprocating mass is ω²r·cosθ, giving an inertia force mω²r cosθ along the stroke. This is the largest shaking force. It repeats once per revolution (hence 'primary'). It is partially balanced by a counterweight, but complete cancellation along the stroke direction is impossible with a rotating mass."),
    P("What is the secondary force in a reciprocating engine?", "The secondary inertia force F_s = m·ω²·r·(cos 2θ)/n, where n = connecting-rod length / crank radius (l/r); it repeats twice per revolution and is smaller than the primary force.", "Secondary force = mω²r(cos2θ)/n — correction for finite con-rod length.", "Because the connecting rod is not infinite, the piston acceleration carries an extra term ω²r·cos2θ/n. The secondary force mω²r cos2θ/n is 1/n of the primary and varies at twice crank speed. It cannot be balanced by a single counter-rotating mass; the Lanchester balancer (two counter-rotating eccentrics) is used for secondary balancing."),
    P("What is hammer blow in railway locomotive balancing?", "Hammer blow is the maximum vertical (or horizontal) fluctuation of the rail force caused by the rotating balance mass: H = m_b·ω²·r, acting when the balance weight is horizontal.", "Hammer blow = m_bω²r — the fluctuating track force from the balance mass.", "In a locomotive wheel, partial balance masses create a vertical centrifugal component m_bω²r sinθ that pushes the wheel down and up once per revolution. Its maximum value (m_bω²r) is the hammer blow. Railway regulations limit it (typically a fraction of the static wheel load) to prevent track damage. Balancing proportion is chosen so that both hammer blow and residual provided improvements to unbalance are acceptable."),
    P("Why is only partial balancing possible for reciprocating masses?", "A rotating balance mass produces a rotating centrifugal force, but the reciprocating mass has a reciprocating (one-directional) inertia force; balance along the stroke creates an equal unbalanced force perpendicular to the stroke. Hence only partial (a fraction c) balancing is possible.", "Reciprocating force is directional; rotating mass gives rotating balance — complete cancellation impossible.", "The reciprocating inertia force mω²r cosθ acts only along the stroke. A rotating mass of fraction c produces a force with components c·mω²r cosθ (along stroke) and c·mω²r sinθ (perpendicular). Balancing the primary along the stroke fully creates a transverse force of the same size; to keep both components acceptable, about two-thirds of the primary is balanced."),
    P("What is the Lanchester balancer?", "The Lanchester balancer cancels the secondary reciprocating force using two equal countershafts rotating at twice crank speed in opposite directions, whose combined vertical/side components cancel the secondary force.", "Two counter-rotating weights at 2ω cancel the cos2θ force.", "The secondary force mω²r(cos2θ)/n varies at twice engine speed. Two identical eccentrics geared to run at 2ω in opposite directions produce a resultant reciprocating force along the cylinder axis; oriented correctly it cancels the secondary force. Inline engines commonly use such harmonic balancers in the crankshaft pulley."),
    P("What is a gyroscopic couple?", "When a spinning disc (moment of inertia I, spin ω) has its axis precessed at angular velocity ω_p, a gyroscopic couple C = I·ω·ω_p acts, trying to turn the body about the third (mutually perpendicular) axis.", "Gyroscopic couple C = I·ω·ω_p, perpendicular to both spin and precession.", "The vector product C = I(ω × ω_p) shows the couple lies perpendicular to the spin and precession axes. For example, a spinning top precessing does not fall because the couple supports its weight. In engineering, the gyroscopic couple appears when ships, aircraft, and rotors change heading or attitude, and it must be accounted for in bearing and structural loads."),
    P("What is the gyroscopic effect on a ship when steering?", "During steering (turn about the vertical axis), the gyroscopic couple acts about the transverse axis, tending to raise or lower the bow and stern (pitching). The direction depends on the rotor spin direction and the turning sense.", "Steering → couple about the transverse axis (pitching effect).", "For a ship with the rotor spinning clockwise viewed from the stern, turning right produces a couple that tends to raise the bow and lower the stern (or the reverse sense depending on conventions). The magnitude C = Iωω_p where ω_p is the yaw rate. The hull and bearings see these transient couples."),
    P("What is the gyroscopic effect on a ship during pitching?", "During pitching (rotation about a transverse horizontal axis), the gyroscopic couple acts about the longitudinal axis, so the ship tends to roll.", "Pitching → couple about the longitudinal (roll) axis.", "When the ship pitches, its rotor is precessed about the transverse axis at the pitch rate; the couple Iωω_p is then about the fore-aft (roll) axis, tending to roll the ship. The rolling tendency changes direction as pitching goes up or down. Pitching couples matter on small fast ships more than steering couples."),
    P("What is the gyroscopic effect on an aeroplane when it turns?", "When an aeroplane turns (yaws), the propeller's gyroscopic couple acts about the transverse axis, tending to pitch the nose up or down depending on the propeller spin and direction of the turn: C = I·ω·ω_p.", "Turning the aircraft → nose-up/down couple from the propeller gyro.", "For a right-hand-turning propeller, yawing to the left produces an upward nose couple and yawing right a downward one (if viewed from the cockpit), because the precession axis is the yaw axis. Slow turns give small couples, but fast turns or spins can create significant pitching moments, especially in light aircraft."),
    P("What is the gyroscopic effect on naval ship stability (rolling)?", "A ship's gyrostabiliser uses a spinning wheel whose precession (pitching) produces a restoring (roll) couple that opposes the wave-induced rolling motion of the ship.", "A gyro-stabiliser counters rolling by generating a roll couple from pitching.", "Mounting a gyroscope whose spin axis is athwartship, wave-induced pitching precesses it and generates a roll-correcting couple Iωω_p. This couples and dampens rolls. Used on small naval and passenger vessels. If the control is miscalculated the gyro can add to the roll instead of opposing it, so damped feedback is essential."),
    P("What are free, forced, and damped vibrations?", "Free vibration: a system oscillates at its natural frequency after an initial disturbance with no sustained input. Forced vibration: maintained by a periodic external force at its frequency. Damped vibration: energy is dissipated by friction/damping so amplitude decays.", "Free (natural), forced (driven), damped (energy loss).", "After an impulse, an undamped system vibrates forever at ω_n. In practice damping removes energy exponentially. With periodic forcing, the steady state oscillates at the driving frequency with amplitude set by the magnification factor; near resonance the amplitude peaks. The equation m x'' + c x' + k x = F(t) covers all three cases."),
    P("What is resonance in the dynamics of machines?", "Resonance is the condition when the frequency of the applied periodic force equals a natural frequency of the system, causing the amplitude of vibration to grow to very large (idealized infinite) values limited only by damping.", "Forcing frequency = natural frequency → maximum response.", "At resonance the denominator of the amplitude expression is at its minimum, so small forces produce large displacements (e.g., soldiers breaking step on a bridge, resonant machine foundations). Damping finite at resonance limits the amplitude to X = F_0/(cω_n) and the magnification to 1/(2ζ). Resonances are avoided by changing speed, stiffness or mass."),
    P("What is the natural frequency of a spring-mass system?", "For a mass m on a spring of stiffness k, ω_n = √(k/m) rad/s, f_n = (1/2π)√(k/m) Hz. Also ω_n = √(g/δ_st) with δ_st the static deflection.", "ω_n = √(k/m) = √(g/δ_st).", "The spring-mass oscillator has natural frequency ω_n = √(k/m). Since the static deflection δ_st = mg/k, the same result reads ω_n = √(g/δ_st) — a very handy form: 1 mm static deflection gives about 15.8 Hz. Increasing stiffness or reducing mass raises the frequency. It is the basis of machine isolation."),
    P("What is the natural frequency of a spring system in series and in parallel?", "Springs in series: 1/k_eq = 1/k₁ + 1/k₂ → softer. In parallel: k_eq = k₁ + k₂ → stiffer. Then ω_n = √(k_eq/m).", "Series softens, parallel stiffens; both change ω_n = √(k_eq/m).", "For n springs in series the equivalent stiffness is lower than any single spring: k_eq = 1/(Σ1/k_i); the natural frequency falls. In parallel k_eq = Σk_i and frequency rises. Example: two equal springs of stiffness k: series k_eq = k/2, parallel 2k — a factor of 2 in frequency between the two arrangements."),
    P("What is the natural frequency of a cantilever beam with an end mass?", "For a cantilever of length L, modulus E, inertia I, with end mass m: ω_n = √(3EI/(mL³)) rad/s (beam mass neglected).", "Cantilever: ω_n = √(3EI/mL³) using the end-load stiffness.", "The tip stiffness of a cantilever is k = 3EI/L³ (from δ = WL³/3EI), so with an end mass the natural frequency is ω_n = √(3EI/(mL³)). Including the beam's own mass, an equivalent fraction (~0.24 of the beam mass) is added to the end mass for the fundamental mode. This models shafts, blades, and springs."),
    P("What is the natural frequency of a simply supported beam with a central load?", "For a simply supported beam of span L carrying a central load equivalent mass m: ω_n = √(48EI/(mL³)) rad/s, using the mid-span stiffness 48EI/L³.", "SS beam: ω_n = √(48EI/mL³) from mid-span stiffness k = 48EI/L³.", "The central deflection of a simply supported beam under load W is WL³/48EI, so the equivalent stiffness at mid-span is k = 48EI/L³. A central mass m then has ω_n = √(48EI/(mL³)). Including beam weight, about 17/35 of the beam mass is added at the centre. This governs the fundamental bending frequency of shafts between supports."),
    P("What is the whirling of shafts?", "Whirling is the violent lateral vibration of a rotating shaft that occurs at the critical speed, when the rotational speed equals the shaft's natural bending frequency; unbalance then drives a large deflection.", "Whirling occurs at the critical speed = natural bending frequency.", "Every shaft-disc system has a lateral natural frequency ω_n = √(k/m). When the rotational speed ω matches ω_n, the centrifugal force of the unbalance can keep growing deflection — the shaft whirls (whips) with large amplitude. Critical speed f_c = (1/2π)√(g/δ_st) where δ_st is the static sag. Operation near it is avoided; stiff short shafts have high critical speeds."),
    P("How is the critical speed of a rotor found?", "N_c = 60 × (1/2π)√(g/δ_st) rpm, where δ_st is the static deflection sag under the disc weight. Equivalently f_c = √(g/δ_st)/2π Hz.", "Critical speed: f_c = (1/2π)√(g/δ_st).", "For a single-disc rotor, the whirling critical speed equals the natural frequency: ω_c = √(g/δ_st). Multiply by (1/2π) for Hz and by 60 for rpm: N_c = 60/(2π)·√(g/δ_st). A stiffer (smaller sag) shaft has a higher critical speed. Multi-disc shafts have several critical speeds, one per bending mode."),
    P("What is vibration isolation?", "Vibration isolation is the reduction of transmitted force or displacement between a machine and its support using resilient mounts (springs, rubber, cork) so that the transmissibility becomes less than unity.", "Isolate by choosing frequency ratio above √2 so the transmitted force falls.", "A machine on flexible mounts transmits a fraction TR of its unbalanced force to the foundation. When the running frequency ω is more than √2 times the natural frequency of the mount system (ω/ω_n > √2), the transmissibility is below 1 and real isolation occurs. Isolators are therefore tuned so that the mount natural frequency is well below the forcing frequency (soft mounts)."),
    P("What is transmissibility (TR)?", "Transmissibility is the ratio of the transmitted force (or displacement) to the applied force: TR = √(1 + (2ζr)²)/√((1–r²)² + (2ζr)²), r = ω/ω_n. TR < 1 only for r > √2.", "TR = transmitted/impressed; isolation zone r > √2.", "Transmissibility measures how much of the machine unbalance force reaches the base. At resonance it peaks (TR ≈ 1/(2ζ)); between r = 0 and √2 the mounts amplify; above r = √2 the transmission drops below 1, so isolation improves as the frequency ratio grows. Damping lowers the resonant peak but raises TR slightly in the isolation region."),
    P("What is the magnification factor in forced vibration?", "The magnification factor M = X/(F_0/k) = 1/√((1–r²)² + (2ζr)²), the ratio of the dynamic displacement amplitude to the static deflection under the force amplitude.", "M = 1/√((1–r²)²+(2ζr)²); at resonance M = 1/(2ζ).", "For a sinusoidally forced damped oscillator, M multiplies the static deflection F_0/k to give the amplitude. At resonance (r = 1), M = 1/(2ζ): with 5% damping M ≈ 10. For r > √2 the response is smaller than static, which is why high-frequency excitation causes little vibration. M is used to size machine foundations."),
    P("What is the damping ratio ζ?", "The damping ratio is ζ = c/c_c = c/(2√(km)) = c/(2mω_n). If ζ < 1 the system is underdamped (oscillates); ζ = 1 critically damped; ζ > 1 overdamped (no oscillation).", "ζ = c/(2√(km)); classifies under, critical, over damping.", "Critical damping c_c = 2√(km) = 2mω_n is the least damping giving non-oscillatory return. ζ measures the actual damping in these units: underdamped (ζ<1) systems vibrate with decaying sinusoid; critically damped (ζ=1) return fastest without overshoot; overdamped (ζ>1) respond sluggishly. Machine isolators typically have ζ = 0.05–0.2."),
    P("What is logarithmic decrement?", "The logarithmic decrement δ = ln(x₁/x₂) is the natural logarithm of the ratio of successive amplitudes of a free damped oscillation; δ = 2πζ/√(1–ζ²), and for small damping ζ ≈ δ/(2π).", "δ = ln(x_n/x_n+1) ≈ 2πζ for small damping.", "In free damped vibration the successive peak amplitudes decay geometrically as e^(–ζω_nT). The constant ratio means ln(x_n/x_{n+1}) = δ ≈ 2πζ. Measuring the amplitude decay of a free vibration (decay test) therefore gives ζ directly: ζ = δ/√(4π² + δ²). This is the standard experimental method for damping."),
    P("What are the types of vibration in machine elements?", "Vibration is classified by motion as longitudinal, transverse (lateral), and torsional; by source as free/forced; and by damping as undamped or damped. Continuous members have infinite natural frequencies.", "Longitudinal, transverse, torsional — plus free/forced and damped/undamped.", "Longitudinal vibration is along the member axis (rods, springs); transverse is transverse (beams, shafts); torsional involves twist oscillation (shafts). Each system has natural frequencies derived from its stiffness and inertia, and all can resonate under periodic excitation. Gear boxes, engines, and drives experience all three types."),
    P("What are the degrees of freedom of a vibrating system?", "The number of degrees of freedom of a vibrating system is the number of independent coordinates required to describe its motion uniquely.", "DOF = number of independent coordinates for the motion.", "A single spring-mass has 1 DOF; a two-disc torsional system has 2 DOF; a continuous beam has infinitely many DOF. The number of natural frequencies equals the number of DOF. Lumped-parameter models approximate continuous systems as finite-DOF, which is adequate for most isolation and frequency design."),
    P("What is simple harmonic motion (SHM)?", "SHM is periodic motion in which the acceleration is proportional to and opposite in direction to the displacement from the mean position: x = A sin(ωt + φ), with period T = 2π/ω and acceleration a = –ω²x.", "x = A sin(ωt); a = –ω²x — restoring, proportional.", "In SHM the displacement, velocity (Aω cos ωt) and acceleration (–Aω² sin ωt) are sinusoidal. Energy oscillates between kinetic and potential with constant total ½kA². Any spring-mass system at its natural frequency moves in SHM. SHM is the building block of Fourier representation of vibration."),
    P("What is the natural frequency of longitudinal vibration of a uniform rod?", "For a uniform bar of length L, density ρ and modulus E: fundamental frequency f = (1/2L)√(E/ρ) for free-free ends and f = (1/4L)√(E/ρ) for fixed-free ends.", "Longitudinal rod: f = (1/2L)√(E/ρ) (free-free) or (1/4L)√(E/ρ) (fixed-free).", "The longitudinal wave speed in a bar is c = √(E/ρ). The fundamental mode has a half wavelength spanning the bar for free-free ends (f = c/2L) and a quarter wavelength for fixed-free (f = c/4L). Steel (E = 200 GPa, ρ = 7800) gives c ≈ 5060 m/s, so a 1 m free-free bar has f ≈ 2530 Hz."),
    P("What is torsional vibration of a shaft?", "Torsional vibration is the angular oscillation of a shaft-twist system. For a uniform shaft of stiffness GJ/L with an end disc of polar inertia I_p: ω_n = √(GJ/(I_p·L)).", "Torsional: ω_n = √(GJ/(I_p L)) for a shaft with end disc.", "Twisting and releasing a shaft-disc system produces angular oscillation. The stiffness is GJ/L and the inertia I_p, so ω_n = √(GJ/(I_pL)). Multi-disc drivelines (crankshafts, propeller shafts) show several torsional modes; critical-speed mapping of torsional vibrations is essential in engine design to avoid crankshaft torsional resonance."),
    P("What is critical damping?", "Critical damping is the minimum damping that allows the system to return to rest without oscillation: c_c = 2√(km) = 2mω_n.", "c_c = 2√(km) = 2mω_n — fastest non-oscillatory return.", "For the equation mx'' + cx' + kx = 0, the discriminant of the characteristic equation is zero at c = 2√(km). At critical damping the response decays to zero in the shortest time without overshoot (no oscillation). Suspension dampers and instrument pointers are tuned to a fraction of critical damping, typically ζ ≈ 0.5–0.7 for suspension feel."),
    P("What is the effect of damping at resonance?", "At resonance the amplitude is finite because of damping: X = F_0/(cω_n), magnification M = 1/(2ζ); increasing damping reduces the resonant peak and broadens the response, but it slightly raises transmissibility in the isolation region.", "Damping caps the resonant peak at 1/(2ζ) and widens the band; it harms isolation at high r.", "Without damping, resonance would give unbounded amplitude. With ζ, the peak magnification is 1/(2ζ) — 10 at 5% damping. Above r = √2, damping raises TR modestly, so isolation design uses the least damping that still suppresses the peak. Hence isolators use a compromise of stiffness and damping."),
    P("What is the Rayleigh method for natural frequency?", "Rayleigh's method equates the maximum kinetic and potential energies of an assumed deflection mode to estimate the fundamental frequency: ω_n² = (∫EI(y'')² dx)/(∫m y² dx).", "Rayleigh: set PE_max = KE_max using an assumed mode shape to get ω².", "For a beam, the fundamental frequency is bounded by ω_n² = (∫EI (y'')² dx)/(∫ρA y² dx), with the statically deflected shape as y. The estimate is an upper bound and is very accurate when the assumed shape is close to the true mode. Rayleigh's energy method is standard for beams, plates, and shafts with distributed mass."),
    P("What is the crank and slotted lever quick-return mechanism?", "In the crank and slotted lever mechanism, the crank pin slides in a slot of a lever that oscillates about a fixed pivot; the return stroke is quicker. The time ratio (cutting/return) = (2π – 2α)/(2α), where α is the swing angle of the lever beyond the vertical.", "Crank-slotted lever: shaper mechanism; quick return ratio = (π–α)/α.", "The crank and slotted lever mechanism used in shaping machines: the drive crank rotates fully, its pin moving the slotted lever. The lever swings through angle 2α; the crank spends (π+α)/ω on the cutting stroke and (π–α)/ω on return. Quick return ratio QRR = (π + α)/(π – α) for the time of cutting over return."),
    P("What is the transmission angle of a four-bar mechanism?", "The transmission angle is the angle between the coupler and the follower at the follower joint. A transmission angle near 90° gives smooth force transmission; below about 40–50° mechaنism becomes poor or locks.", "Transmission angle: angle between coupler and follower; keep it near 90°.", "The transmission angle μ measures how well the coupler force drives the follower: the tangential component of the coupler force is proportional to sin μ. Values far from 90° degrade the mechanical advantage and cause jamming. Design constrains μ_min (typically ≥ 40°) across the entire motion range. Grashof mechanisms usually meet this requirement."),
    P("What is the rubbing velocity at a pin joint?", "The rubbing velocity at a pin joint is v_r = ω × r_pin, where ω is the relative angular velocity of the two links and r_pin the pin radius. It determines the bearing surface wear at the joint.", "Rubbing velocity = relative angular velocity × pin radius.", "At a turning pair, the two links rotate relative to each other about the pin axis at angular velocity ω_rel; the points of contact slide at speed ω_rel × r. Summing over pin joints gives the total rubbing velocity, which is minimized to reduce wear. It appears in wear/power-loss calculations of mechanisms."),
    P("What is the analytical formula for piston acceleration in a slider-crank?", "For crank radius r, angular speed ω, con-rod length l (n = l/r): a = –ω²r(cosθ + cos2θ/n), θ from TDC. The velocity v = ωr(sinθ + sin2θ/(2n)).", "Piston acceleration = –ω²r(cosθ + cos2θ/n).", "The exact piston position from TDC is x = r(1 – cosθ) + l(1 – √(1 – (sinθ/n)²)). Expanding the root and differentiating: v ≈ ωr(sinθ + sin2θ/(2n)) and a ≈ –ω²r(cosθ + cos2θ/n). The second term is the correction due to the finite connecting rod; n = l/r is typically 3–5 for engines."),
    P("What is a Hooke's (universal) joint and its velocity fluctuation?", "A Hooke's joint connects two intersecting shafts; with angle α between shafts, the speed ratio ω_out/ω_in = cosα/(1 – sin²α cos²θ) varies between cosα and 1/cosα twice per revolution, unless compensated by a second joint.", "Universal joint: speed fluctuates with driven angle; two joints cancel it.", "For a single universal joint at shaft angle α and driver angle θ: tanθ_out = tanθ/cosα, giving ω_out/ω_in = cosα/(1 – sin²α cos²θ). The driven shaft speed fluctuates twice per revolution between ωcosα and ω/cosα. Using two Hooke's joints in series (or a homokinetic joint) removes this fluctuation. Used in propeller shafts of vehicles."),
    P("Compare open belt and crossed belt drives.", "Open belt drive connects pulleys rotating in the same direction; the arc of contact can exceed 180° on the smaller pulley only by moving the pulleys closer. Crossed belt reverses rotation and gives larger wrap angles on both pulleys, increasing power capacity but with more wear at the crossing point.", "Open: same rotation; crossed: opposite rotation and bigger wrap.", "In an open belt, the two sides run parallel and the pulleys turn the same way; the small pulley wrap angle is smaller (180° minus an angle depending on the centre distance). In a crossed belt, the slack sides cross, reversing the direction and raising the angle of contact on both pulleys to over 180°, which improves the grip and power, at the cost of chafing where the strands cross."),
    P("What is the purpose of an idler (jockey) pulley?", "An idler pulley, placed on the slack side, increases the angle of contact (wrap), maintains tension, and prevents sagging, raising the power-transmitting capacity of the belt drive.", "Idler increases wrap angle and tension on the slack side.", "The angle of lap limits a belt's driving capacity through the ratio e^(μθ). Adding an idler pulley to the slack side increases θ on the main pulleys and removes the sag, so the drive transmits more power without raising the initial tension. Jockey pulleys also help when the centres are short or the ratio is large."),
    P("What is a rack and pinion? What is its purpose?", "A rack is a gear of infinite pitch-circle radius; the pinion meshing with a rack converts rotation into straight-line motion (or vice versa) with a stroke equal to the pinion's travel along the rack.", "Rack = infinite-radius gear; converts rotation to translation.", "If a spur gear's radius becomes infinite, the pitch circle straightens into the rack's pitch line. Meshing a pinion with the rack converts rotary motion to linear motion in steered shafts, machine-tool tables, and hydraulic cylinder replacement drives. One pinion revolution advances the rack by πd = πmN (d = pinion pitch diameter)."),
    P("What are the advantages of helical gears over spur gears?", "Helical gears have teeth inclined to the axis, giving gradual engagement (higher load capacity, smoother and quieter running, less vibration), but they produce axial thrust; double-helical (herringbone) gears cancel the thrust.", "Helical: inclined teeth, smooth gradual mesh, but axial thrust; herringbone cancels it.", "With teeth inclined at helix angle ψ, the contact begins gradually, engaging along a line that sweeps, so helical gears are smoother, quieter, and can carry more load for the same size. The axial thrust component F_a = F_t·tanψ must be taken by the bearings; double helical (herringbone) gears introduce equal and opposite thrust to cancel it."),
    P("What are bevel gears and worm gears?", "Bevel gears transmit power between intersecting (often perpendicular) shafts; worm and worm-wheel drives transmit between non-intersecting perpendicular shafts with a large reduction ratio (10–80:1).", "Bevel: intersecting axes; worm: crossed axes, large reduction, self-locking.", "Bevel gears mesh on conical surfaces; straight, spiral or hypoid teeth connect intersecting shafts, e.g., differentials. A worm gear resembles a screw meshing with a helical wheel; each turn advances the wheel by one tooth, giving large speed reduction. Worm drives are compact, quiet, and can be self-locking (driving only one way), but efficiency is lower."),
    P("What is rolling resistance (rolling friction)?", "Rolling resistance is the resistance to motion of a rolling wheel caused by the deformation of the wheel and the ground; F_r = C_r·N, where C_r is the rolling resistance coefficient (typically 0.01–0.02).", "Rolling resistance: deformation-based; F = C_r N, C_r very small.", "When a wheel rolls, the contact patch deforms and the normal reaction shifts forward, creating a resisting moment. The force opposing rolling is F_r = (deformation arm/radius)·N = C_r·N, with C_r notably smaller than the sliding friction coefficient. Hard surfaces give small C_r (steel on steel ≈ 0.001; rubber on road ≈ 0.015), which is why wheels are far more efficient than sliding."),
    P("State Petroff's equation for journal-bearing friction.", "For a lightly loaded journal bearing, Petroff's equation is T = 2πμ N L r³/c (torque) or μ_f = 2π²(r/c)(μN_shore/P), where r = journal radius, c = radial clearance, L = length, N = speed, μ = viscosity.", "Petroff: friction in a concentric journal ≈ 2π²(r/c)(μN/P).", "Treating the clearance annulus as a thin fluid film with Couette flow, Petroff's equation estimates the friction torque T = 2πμNLr³/c and the coefficient of friction μ_f = 2π²(r/c)(μN/P). It is valid for light loads where the journal stays nearly concentric (high speed, low load) and is the starting point of hydrodynamic lubrication analysis."),
    P("Compare belt, chain, and gear drives.", "Belts: smooth, cheap, shock-absorbing, but slip and limited torque. Chains: positive (no slip), medium power over long centres, need lubrication. Gears: exact ratio, highest torque and efficiency in compact space, but require precise alignment and lubrication.", "Belts flexible/slipping; chains positive over long distances; gears precise and compact but rigid.", "Belts transmit over long distances quietly and tolerate shock and misalignment but slip and have limited power. Chains give positive, slip-free transmission over moderate-to-long centres with good efficiency but need lubrication and wear by elongation. Gears provide the most precise velocity ratio, highest capacity, and smallest package, but demand accurate centres, shafts, and lubrication — the choice depends on ratio, power, centres, and cost."),
];
  if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM;
  if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM = SSC_JE_ENC_SOMTOM;
})();
